window.WB_BOOK=window.WB_BOOK||{};window.WB_BOOK["week06"]={"id":"week06","n":6,"section":"B","label":"","title":"Gas Laws, Heat Transfer & Checkpoint Test","titleText":"Gas Laws, Heat Transfer & Checkpoint Test","subtitle":"Absolute zero, Boyle, Charles and the pressure law — conduction, convection, radiation — and a 40-mark test on Sections A & B","intro":"This week finishes Section B. First you meet the gas laws: how the pressure, volume and temperature of a fixed mass of gas are linked, why there is a lowest possible temperature (absolute zero, −273 °C) and why we must use the Kelvin scale in every gas calculation. You will explain each law using moving particles (kinetic theory). Then you learn the three ways thermal energy travels — conduction, convection and radiation — the classic experiments, and how they explain the vacuum flask, the solar water heater, the greenhouse and the cooking pot. The week ends with a 40-mark Checkpoint Test on everything from Week 1 to Week 6. Do the test under exam conditions (60 minutes, no notes) so your tutor can see exactly which topics need more work before the mock in December.","objectives":[{"code":"B2.9","text":"Use graphs of pressure or volume against temperature to define absolute zero"},{"code":"B2.10","text":"Convert temperatures between the Celsius and Kelvin scales ⟪(T/K=\\theta /{}^{\\circ}\\mathrm{C}+273)⟫"},{"code":"B2.11","text":"Apply Boyle's law, Charles' law, the pressure law and the general gas law (pV/T = constant) to solve problems"},{"code":"B2.12","text":"Explain the gas laws using the kinetic theory of gases"},{"code":"B4.1","text":"Explain the transfer of thermal energy by conduction"},{"code":"B4.2","text":"Explain the transfer of thermal energy by convection"},{"code":"B4.3","text":"Explain the transfer of thermal energy by radiation"},{"code":"B4.4","text":"Conduct experiments to investigate the factors on which the absorption and emission of radiation depend"},{"code":"B4.5","text":"Recall that good absorbers of radiation are also good emitters"},{"code":"B4.6","text":"Relate heat transfer to everyday applications (vacuum flask, solar water heater, greenhouse, cooking utensils)"}],"formulaCard":[{"tex":"T=\\theta +273","meaning":"Kelvin temperature from Celsius; ⟪\\theta =T-273⟫","units":"K, °C"},{"tex":"\\text{Absolute zero }=0\\,\\mathrm{K}=-273\\,{}^{\\circ}\\mathrm{C}","meaning":"Lowest possible temperature; particles have minimum KE","units":"K"},{"tex":"p_{1}V_{1}=p_{2}V_{2}","meaning":"Boyle's law (constant T, fixed mass)","units":"any, same both sides"},{"tex":"V_{1}/T_{1}=V_{2}/T_{2}","meaning":"Charles' law (constant p); T in K","units":"T in K"},{"tex":"p_{1}/T_{1}=p_{2}/T_{2}","meaning":"Pressure law (constant V); T in K","units":"T in K"},{"tex":"p_{1}V_{1}/T_{1}=p_{2}V_{2}/T_{2}","meaning":"General gas law (fixed mass)","units":"T in K"},{"tex":"\\text{Absolute zero from graph: }\\theta =-c\\div m","meaning":"c = intercept at 0 °C, m = gradient","units":"°C"},{"text":"Conduction / Convection / Radiation","meaning":"particle vibrations + free electrons / fluid moves (density) / IR waves, no medium","units":""},{"text":"Dull black: best absorber AND best emitter","meaning":"Shiny silver: best reflector, worst absorber and emitter","units":""},{"text":"Vacuum flask","meaning":"vacuum → conduction & convection; silvering → radiation; stopper → convection, evaporation, conduction","units":""}],"glossary":[{"term":"Absolute zero","def":"The lowest possible temperature, 0 K or −273 °C; particles have minimum kinetic energy."},{"term":"Kelvin scale","def":"Absolute temperature scale starting at absolute zero; 1 K step ⟪=1\\,{}^{\\circ}\\mathrm{C}⟫ step."},{"term":"Extrapolate","def":"Extend a graph line beyond the measured points (drawn dashed)."},{"term":"Boyle's law","def":"⟪p\\propto 1/V⟫ for a fixed mass of gas at constant temperature."},{"term":"Charles' law","def":"⟪V\\propto T⟫ (kelvin) for a fixed mass of gas at constant pressure."},{"term":"Pressure law","def":"⟪p\\propto T⟫ (kelvin) for a fixed mass of gas at constant volume."},{"term":"General gas law","def":"pV/T = constant for a fixed mass of gas."},{"term":"Bourdon gauge","def":"A dial instrument that measures gas pressure."},{"term":"Conduction","def":"Heat transfer through a material by particle vibrations (and free electrons), without the material moving."},{"term":"Convection","def":"Heat transfer in a fluid by movement of the fluid itself, due to density differences."},{"term":"Convection current","def":"Circulating flow: hot, less dense fluid rises; cool, denser fluid sinks."},{"term":"Radiation","def":"Heat transfer by infrared electromagnetic waves; needs no medium."},{"term":"Insulator (thermal)","def":"A poor conductor of heat, e.g. wood, plastic, trapped air."},{"term":"Leslie cube","def":"Metal cube with different surfaces, used to compare emission of radiation."},{"term":"Thermopile","def":"A detector of infrared radiation, connected to a galvanometer."},{"term":"Greenhouse effect","def":"Trapping of long-wavelength infrared by glass or atmospheric gases, warming the space beneath."},{"term":"Thermosiphon","def":"Circulation of water by convection (no pump), as in a solar water heater."}],"studyPlan":[["Day after session","Re-read Units 6.1–6.3 notes; copy the four gas-law formulas onto your formula card","30 min",""],["Day 2","Practice questions Units 6.1–6.3 (gas laws)","45 min",""],["Day 3","Units 6.4–6.6 notes + practice (heat transfer) + Exam-Level MCQs","45 min",""],["Day 4","Revise Weeks 1–5 formula cards for 20 min, then sit the CHECKPOINT TEST (Unit 6.7): 60 min, no notes, timed","80 min",""],["Every day","Flash cards: definitions + formulas (all weeks so far)","5–10 min",""]],"lessons":[{"id":"week06.6.1","kind":"unit","unit":"6.1","title":"Absolute Zero and the Kelvin Scale","titleText":"Absolute Zero and the Kelvin Scale","goals":[{"code":"B2.9","text":"Use graphs of pressure or volume against temperature to define absolute zero"},{"code":"B2.10","text":"Convert temperatures between the Celsius and Kelvin scales ⟪(T/K=\\theta /{}^{\\circ}\\mathrm{C}+273)⟫"}],"codes":["B2.9","B2.10"],"words":[{"term":"Absolute zero","def":"The lowest possible temperature, 0 K or −273 °C; particles have minimum kinetic energy."},{"term":"Kelvin scale","def":"Absolute temperature scale starting at absolute zero; 1 K step ⟪=1\\,{}^{\\circ}\\mathrm{C}⟫ step."},{"term":"Extrapolate","def":"Extend a graph line beyond the measured points (drawn dashed)."}],"learn":{"notes":[{"type":"h","text":"What happens to a gas when you cool it?"},{"type":"p","text":"Take a fixed mass of gas (the same amount of air, sealed in). If you keep its **pressure constant** and cool it, its **volume gets smaller**. If you keep its **volume constant** and cool it, its **pressure gets smaller**. In both cases the graph against Celsius temperature is a **straight line**."},{"type":"img","src":"img/week06/abs_zero","alt":"Figure 6.1 — Volume of a fixed mass of gas against Celsius temperature. Extending (extrapolating) the line backwards, it reaches zero volume at about −273 °C.","caption":"Figure 6.1 — Volume of a fixed mass of gas against Celsius temperature. Extending (extrapolating) the line backwards, it reaches zero volume at about −273 °C.","width":5,"w":1094,"h":653},{"type":"p","text":"If we **extrapolate** (extend) the straight line backwards, it always meets the temperature axis at about **−273 °C**, whatever gas we use and whatever amount. At this temperature the gas would have zero volume (or zero pressure). Real gases turn into liquids before this, so that part of the line is drawn **dashed**."},{"type":"def","term":"Absolute zero","text":"the lowest possible temperature, ⟪-273\\,{}^{\\circ}\\mathrm{C}\\ (0\\,\\mathrm{K})⟫, at which the particles of a substance have the minimum possible kinetic energy. It is the temperature at which the pressure (or volume) of an ideal gas would become zero."},{"type":"remember","text":"Nothing can be colder than absolute zero. At 0 K the particles have their LEAST possible energy — they cannot slow down any more. (A more exact value is −273.15 °C, but CSEC uses −273 °C.)"},{"type":"h","text":"The Kelvin (absolute) temperature scale"},{"type":"p","text":"Lord Kelvin suggested a scale that starts at absolute zero. One kelvin is the **same size** as one degree Celsius — only the starting point is different."},{"type":"img","src":"img/week06/kelvin_scale","alt":"Figure 6.2 — Comparing the Celsius and Kelvin scales","caption":"Figure 6.2 — Comparing the Celsius and Kelvin scales","width":4.6,"w":931,"h":574},{"type":"formula","tex":"T=\\theta +273\\qquad \\theta =T-273","where":["⟪T⟫ = temperature on the Kelvin scale, in kelvin (K)","⟪\\theta⟫ = temperature on the Celsius scale, in degrees Celsius (°C)"],"label":"CONVERTING TEMPERATURE"},{"type":"table","headers":["Temperature","Celsius (°C)","Kelvin (K)"],"rows":[["Absolute zero","−273","0"],["Ice point (pure melting ice)","0","273"],["A cool room","20","293"],["Room temperature (often used in questions)","27","300"],["Normal body temperature","37","310"],["Steam point (pure boiling water at 1 atm)","100","373"]]},{"type":"warn","text":"Write kelvin as **K** — NOT °K. And a temperature CHANGE is the same number on both scales: a rise of 20 °C is a rise of 20 K. Only add 273 to an actual temperature, never to a change."},{"type":"tip","text":"In ANY gas-law calculation, convert every temperature to kelvin FIRST. Using °C gives a wrong answer and loses most of the marks. (Doubling the Celsius temperature from 10 °C to 20 °C does NOT double the volume; doubling the kelvin temperature does.)"},{"type":"h","text":"Finding absolute zero from a graph (B2.9)"},{"type":"steps","items":["Plot pressure (or volume) on the y-axis against temperature in °C on the x-axis. The x-axis must go back to about −300 °C.","Draw the best-fit straight line through your points.","Extend the line (dashed) until it cuts the temperature axis (where ⟪p=0⟫ or ⟪V=0⟫).","Read off the temperature where it cuts: this is your value for absolute zero (should be close to −273 °C)."]},{"type":"p","text":"You can also calculate it: find the gradient m of the line, read the y-intercept c at 0 °C, then the line reaches zero when ⟪\\boldsymbol{\\theta =-c\\div m}⟫."}],"examples":[{"id":"week06.6.1.e1","title":"Converting temperatures","question":"Convert (a) 27 °C to kelvin, (b) 400 K to °C, (c) −73 °C to kelvin.","steps":[{"label":"(a)","text":"⟪T=\\theta +273=27+273=300\\,\\mathrm{K}⟫"},{"label":"(b)","text":"⟪\\theta =T-273=400-273=127\\,{}^{\\circ}\\mathrm{C}⟫"},{"label":"(c)","text":"⟪T=-73+273=200\\,\\mathrm{K}⟫"}],"answer":"(a) 300 K  (b) 127 °C  (c) 200 K"},{"id":"week06.6.1.e2","title":"Absolute zero from two readings","question":"A fixed mass of gas at constant pressure has volume 30.0 cm^3^ at 0 °C and 41.0 cm^3^ at 100 °C. Assuming a straight-line graph, estimate absolute zero.","img":{"src":"img/week06/abs_zero","alt":"A fixed mass of gas at constant pressure has volume 30.0 cm3 at 0 °C and 41.0 cm3 at 100 °C. Assuming a straight-line graph, estimate absolute zero.","w":1094,"h":653},"steps":[{"label":"Gradient","text":"⟪m=\\Delta V/\\Delta \\theta =(41.0-30.0)\\div (100-0)=11.0\\div 100=0.110\\,\\mathrm{cm}^{3}⟫ per °C"},{"label":"Meaning","text":"The volume falls by 0.110 cm^3^ for every 1 °C of cooling."},{"label":"Cooling needed to reach ⟪V=0⟫","text":"⟪30.0\\div 0.110=273\\,{}^{\\circ}\\mathrm{C}⟫ below 0 °C"},{"label":"Answer","text":"⟪\\theta =0-273=-273\\,{}^{\\circ}\\mathrm{C}⟫"}],"answer":"Absolute zero ≈ ⟪-273\\,{}^{\\circ}\\mathrm{C}\\ (0\\,\\mathrm{K})⟫"},{"id":"week06.6.1.e3","title":"Temperature change","question":"Water is heated from 25 °C to 80 °C. What is the temperature rise (a) in °C, (b) in K?","steps":[{"label":"In °C","text":"⟪\\Delta \\theta =80-25=55\\,{}^{\\circ}\\mathrm{C}⟫"},{"label":"In K","text":"298 K → 353 K, so ⟪\\Delta T=353-298=55\\,\\mathrm{K}⟫. The rise is the same number on both scales."}],"answer":"⟪55\\,{}^{\\circ}\\mathrm{C}=55\\,\\mathrm{K}⟫"}]},"tryit":[{"id":"week06.6.1.p1","kind":"self","q":"Define **absolute zero** and give its value in °C.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":2,"scheme":"The lowest possible temperature, at which particles have minimum kinetic energy / the pressure (volume) of a gas would be zero (1); ⟪-273\\,{}^{\\circ}\\mathrm{C}\\ (0\\,\\mathrm{K})⟫ (1).","points":[{"text":"The lowest possible temperature, at which particles have minimum kinetic energy / the pressure (volume) of a gas would be zero","marks":1},{"text":"⟪-273\\,{}^{\\circ}\\mathrm{C}\\ (0\\,\\mathrm{K})⟫","marks":1}],"section":"try","hint":{"text":"Look back at Example 2 (Absolute zero from two readings). Start like this: Gradient: ⟪m=\\Delta V/\\Delta \\theta =(41.0-30.0)\\div (100-0)=11.0\\div 100=0.110\\,\\mathrm{cm}^{3}⟫ per °C","from":2}},{"id":"week06.6.1.p2","kind":"num","q":"Convert 25 °C to kelvin.","marks":1,"level":1,"lines":1,"scheme":"⟪25+273=298\\,\\mathrm{K}⟫ (1).","points":[{"text":"⟪25+273=298\\,\\mathrm{K}⟫","marks":1}],"num":{"value":298,"sf":3,"unit":"K","unitTex":"\\mathrm{K}"},"section":"try","hint":{"text":"Look back at Example 1 (Converting temperatures). Start like this: (a): ⟪T=\\theta +273=27+273=300\\,\\mathrm{K}⟫","from":1}}],"practice":[{"id":"week06.6.1.p3","kind":"num","q":"Convert 350 K to degrees Celsius.","marks":1,"level":1,"lines":1,"scheme":"⟪350-273=77\\,{}^{\\circ}\\mathrm{C}⟫ (1).","points":[{"text":"⟪350-273=77\\,{}^{\\circ}\\mathrm{C}⟫","marks":1}],"num":{"value":77,"sf":2,"unit":"°C","unitTex":"{}^{\\circ}\\mathrm{C}"},"section":"practice","hint":{"text":"Look back at Example 1 (Converting temperatures). Start like this: (a): ⟪T=\\theta +273=27+273=300\\,\\mathrm{K}⟫","from":1}},{"id":"week06.6.1.p4","kind":"num","q":"Convert −40 °C to kelvin.","marks":1,"level":1,"lines":1,"scheme":"⟪-40+273=233\\,\\mathrm{K}⟫ (1).","points":[{"text":"⟪-40+273=233\\,\\mathrm{K}⟫","marks":1}],"num":{"value":233,"sf":3,"unit":"K","unitTex":"\\mathrm{K}"},"section":"practice","hint":{"text":"Look back at Example 1 (Converting temperatures). Start like this: (a): ⟪T=\\theta +273=27+273=300\\,\\mathrm{K}⟫","from":1}},{"id":"week06.6.1.p5","kind":"num","q":"A liquid warms from 20 °C to 65 °C. What is the rise in temperature in kelvin?","marks":1,"level":1,"lines":1,"scheme":"45 K (a change is the same number on both scales) (1).","points":[{"text":"45 K (a change is the same number on both scales)","marks":1}],"num":{"value":45,"sf":2,"unit":"K","unitTex":"\\mathrm{K}"},"conf":"low","section":"practice","hint":{"text":"Look back at Example 3 (Temperature change). Start like this: In °C: ⟪\\Delta \\theta =80-25=55\\,{}^{\\circ}\\mathrm{C}⟫","from":3}},{"id":"week06.6.1.p6","kind":"self","q":"State ONE similarity and ONE difference between the Celsius and Kelvin scales.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":3,"scheme":"Similarity: one kelvin is the same size as one degree Celsius / same interval between ice and steam points (100) (1). Difference: Kelvin starts at absolute zero ⟪(0\\,\\mathrm{K}=-273\\,{}^{\\circ}\\mathrm{C})⟫ but Celsius 0 is the ice point / Kelvin has no negative values (1).","points":[{"text":"Similarity: one kelvin is the same size as one degree Celsius / same interval between ice and steam points (100)","marks":1},{"text":"Difference: Kelvin starts at absolute zero ⟪(0\\,\\mathrm{K}=-273\\,{}^{\\circ}\\mathrm{C})⟫ but Celsius 0 is the ice point / Kelvin has no negative values","marks":1}],"section":"practice","hint":{"text":"Look back at Example 1 (Converting temperatures). Start like this: (a): ⟪T=\\theta +273=27+273=300\\,\\mathrm{K}⟫","from":1}},{"id":"week06.6.1.p7","kind":"self","q":"Explain why the part of the volume–temperature graph below about −200 °C is drawn as a dashed line.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":3,"scheme":"Real gases condense / turn to liquid (or solid) before reaching absolute zero (1), so measurements cannot be taken there; the line is extrapolated (1).","points":[{"text":"Real gases condense / turn to liquid (or solid) before reaching absolute zero","marks":1},{"text":"so measurements cannot be taken there; the line is extrapolated","marks":1}],"section":"practice","hint":{"text":"Look back at Example 2 (Absolute zero from two readings). Start like this: Gradient: ⟪m=\\Delta V/\\Delta \\theta =(41.0-30.0)\\div (100-0)=11.0\\div 100=0.110\\,\\mathrm{cm}^{3}⟫ per °C","from":2}}],"exit":[{"id":"week06.6.1.p8","kind":"self","q":"A fixed mass of gas at constant pressure has a volume of 25.0 cm^3^ at 0 °C and 34.2 cm^3^ at 100 °C. Calculate (i) the increase in volume per °C, (ii) an estimate of absolute zero.","marks":3,"level":2,"lines":4,"scheme":"(i) ⟪(34.2-25.0)\\div 100=0.092\\,\\mathrm{cm}^{3}⟫ per °C (1). (ii) ⟪25.0\\div 0.092=272\\,{}^{\\circ}\\mathrm{C}⟫ below 0 (1); absolute zero ≈ −272 °C (accept −270 to −275) (1).","points":[{"text":"(i) ⟪(34.2-25.0)\\div 100=0.092\\,\\mathrm{cm}^{3}⟫ per °C","marks":1},{"text":"(ii) ⟪25.0\\div 0.092=272\\,{}^{\\circ}\\mathrm{C}⟫ below 0","marks":1},{"text":"absolute zero ≈ −272 °C (accept −270 to −275)","marks":1}],"section":"exit"},{"id":"week06.6.1.p9","kind":"self","q":"Sketch, on the same axes, the graph of pressure against temperature for a fixed mass of gas at constant volume when temperature is in (i) °C and (ii) K. Label each line.","marks":3,"level":2,"lines":6,"scheme":"(i) °C: straight line with positive gradient that cuts the temperature axis at −273 °C (does not pass through the origin) (1). (ii) K: straight line through the origin (0 K) (1). Both lines parallel / same slope; axes labelled p and θ or T (1).","points":[{"text":"(i) °C: straight line with positive gradient that cuts the temperature axis at −273 °C (does not pass through the origin)","marks":1},{"text":"(ii) K: straight line through the origin (0 K)","marks":1},{"text":"Both lines parallel / same slope; axes labelled p and θ or T","marks":1}],"section":"exit"}],"warmup":[{"id":"week06.6.1.w1","kind":"self","q":"Write the equation for the heat needed to change temperature, and state the unit of c.","marks":1,"level":1,"scheme":"⟪E=mc\\Delta \\theta⟫; c in J kg⁻¹ °C⁻¹ (J kg⁻¹ K⁻¹).","points":[{"text":"⟪E=mc\\Delta \\theta⟫; c in J kg⁻¹ °C⁻¹ (J kg⁻¹ K⁻¹).","marks":1}],"section":"warmup","src":"week05.r1"},{"id":"week06.6.1.w2","kind":"num","q":"How much energy melts 0.20 kg of ice at 0 °C? ⟪(l_{f}=3.4\\times 10^{5}\\,\\mathrm{J}\\,\\mathrm{kg}^{-1})⟫","marks":1,"level":1,"scheme":"⟪E=ml=0.20\\times 3.4\\times 10^{5}=6.8\\times 10^{4}\\,\\mathrm{J}⟫.","points":[{"text":"⟪E=ml=0.20\\times 3.4\\times 10^{5}=6.8\\times 10^{4}\\,\\mathrm{J}⟫.","marks":1}],"num":{"value":68000,"sf":2,"unit":"J","unitTex":"\\mathrm{J}"},"section":"warmup","src":"week05.r2"},{"id":"week06.6.1.w3","kind":"self","q":"Why does the temperature stay constant while a substance melts?","marks":1,"level":1,"scheme":"The energy breaks bonds (increases PE of the particles), not their KE.","points":[{"text":"The energy breaks bonds (increases PE of the particles), not their KE.","marks":1}],"section":"warmup","src":"week05.r3"},{"id":"week06.6.1.w4","kind":"self","q":"State two differences between evaporation and boiling.","marks":1,"level":1,"scheme":"Evaporation: at the surface, any temperature, causes cooling. Boiling: throughout the liquid, fixed temperature, bubbles.","points":[{"text":"Evaporation: at the surface, any temperature, causes cooling. Boiling: throughout the liquid, fixed temperature, bubbles.","marks":1}],"section":"warmup","src":"week05.r4"},{"id":"week06.6.1.w5","kind":"self","q":"Why does a pond freeze from the top down?","marks":1,"level":1,"scheme":"Water is densest at 4 °C and sinks; colder, less dense water stays on top and freezes; ice floats and insulates the water below.","points":[{"text":"Water is densest at 4 °C and sinks; colder, less dense water stays on top and freezes; ice floats and insulates the water below.","marks":1}],"section":"warmup","src":"week05.r5"}],"quickStart":[{"type":"p","text":"Check these four ideas from Week 5 before you start Unit 6.1."},{"type":"h","text":"1. What temperature means (Week 5)"},{"type":"p","text":"Temperature is a measure of the **average kinetic energy** of the particles. Hotter = particles move faster. Thermal energy always flows from a **higher** temperature to a **lower** temperature."},{"type":"h","text":"2. Particles in a gas (Week 5)"},{"type":"bullets","items":["Gas particles are **far apart** and move **randomly at high speed** in all directions.","They collide with each other and with the walls of the container.","The forces between gas particles are almost zero (except during collisions)."]},{"type":"h","text":"3. Pressure (Week 4)"},{"type":"p","text":"Pressure = force ÷ area, ⟪p=F/A⟫. Unit: pascal ⟪(\\mathrm{Pa})=\\mathrm{N}\\,\\mathrm{m}^{-2}⟫. ⟪1\\,\\mathrm{kPa}=1000\\,\\mathrm{Pa}⟫. Atmospheric pressure ≈ ⟪100\\,\\mathrm{kPa}\\ (1.0\\times 10^{5}\\,\\mathrm{Pa})⟫."},{"type":"h","text":"4. Rearranging a ratio equation"},{"type":"p","text":"If ⟪V_{1}/T_{1}=V_{2}/T_{2}⟫, then multiply both sides by T~2~:  ⟪V_{2}=V_{1}\\times T_{2}/T_{1}⟫. **Cross-multiply** and divide — this one skill solves every gas-law question."},{"type":"fill","text":"Try it: make **p~2~** the subject of ⟪p_{1}V_{1}=p_{2}V_{2}⟫.   p~2~ = ","lines":1},{"type":"fill","text":"Try it: make **T~2~** the subject of ⟪p_{1}/T_{1}=p_{2}/T_{2}⟫.   T~2~ = ","lines":1}],"n":38},{"id":"week06.6.2","kind":"unit","unit":"6.2","title":"The Gas Laws: Boyle, Charles, Pressure Law and the General Gas Law","titleText":"The Gas Laws: Boyle, Charles, Pressure Law and the General Gas Law","goals":[{"code":"B2.9","text":"Use graphs of pressure or volume against temperature to define absolute zero"},{"code":"B2.11","text":"Apply Boyle's law, Charles' law, the pressure law and the general gas law (pV/T = constant) to solve problems"}],"codes":["B2.9","B2.11"],"words":[{"term":"Extrapolate","def":"Extend a graph line beyond the measured points (drawn dashed)."},{"term":"Boyle's law","def":"⟪p\\propto 1/V⟫ for a fixed mass of gas at constant temperature."},{"term":"Charles' law","def":"⟪V\\propto T⟫ (kelvin) for a fixed mass of gas at constant pressure."},{"term":"Pressure law","def":"⟪p\\propto T⟫ (kelvin) for a fixed mass of gas at constant volume."},{"term":"General gas law","def":"pV/T = constant for a fixed mass of gas."},{"term":"Bourdon gauge","def":"A dial instrument that measures gas pressure."}],"learn":{"notes":[{"type":"p","text":"A gas has three quantities that can change: **pressure p**, **volume V** and **temperature T**. Each gas law keeps the **mass fixed** and **one quantity constant**, and links the other two."},{"type":"table","headers":["Law","Kept constant","Relationship","Equation","Straight-line graph"],"rows":[["Boyle's law","temperature (and mass)","⟪p\\propto 1/V⟫","⟪p_{1}V_{1}=p_{2}V_{2}⟫","p against 1/V"],["Charles' law","pressure (and mass)","⟪V\\propto T⟫","⟪V_{1}/T_{1}=V_{2}/T_{2}⟫","V against T (K)"],["Pressure law","volume (and mass)","⟪p\\propto T⟫","⟪p_{1}/T_{1}=p_{2}/T_{2}⟫","p against T (K)"],["General gas law","mass only","pV/T = constant","⟪p_{1}V_{1}/T_{1}=p_{2}V_{2}/T_{2}⟫","—"]]},{"type":"h","text":"1. Boyle's law (constant temperature)"},{"type":"def","term":"Boyle's law","text":"the pressure of a fixed mass of gas is inversely proportional to its volume, provided the temperature is kept constant."},{"type":"formula","tex":"p_{1}V_{1}=p_{2}V_{2}\\qquad (pV=\\text{ constant})","where":["⟪p_{1},\\ V_{1}⟫ = pressure and volume at the start;  p~2~, V~2~ = pressure and volume at the end","Any units can be used (kPa, cm^3^…) as long as both sides use the SAME units."],"label":"BOYLE'S LAW"},{"type":"p","text":"**Inversely proportional** means: if the volume is halved, the pressure doubles. If the volume is made 3 times bigger, the pressure becomes one-third."},{"type":"p","text":"**Experiment to verify Boyle's law**"},{"type":"img","src":"img/week06/boyle_apparatus","alt":"Figure 6.3 — Boyle's law apparatus: a column of air trapped above oil in a sealed glass tube","caption":"Figure 6.3 — Boyle's law apparatus: a column of air trapped above oil in a sealed glass tube","width":4.4,"w":1054,"h":778},{"type":"steps","items":["Air is trapped in a sealed glass tube above a column of oil. The length of the air column (read on the scale) is proportional to its volume.","Use the foot pump to push oil up the tube. This compresses the trapped air. Read the pressure on the Bourdon gauge.","**Wait a minute** after each change so the air returns to room temperature (compressing a gas warms it).","Read the volume with your eye **level with the oil surface** (avoid parallax).","Record at least 6 pairs of p and V. Calculate 1/V for each.","Plot p against 1/V."]},{"type":"img","src":"img/week06/boyle_graphs","alt":"Figure 6.4 — Boyle's law graphs: (a) p against V is a curve; (b) p against 1/V is a straight line through the origin","caption":"Figure 6.4 — Boyle's law graphs: (a) p against V is a curve; (b) p against 1/V is a straight line through the origin","width":5.2,"w":1264,"h":553},{"type":"p","text":"**Results and conclusion:** the product ⟪p\\times V⟫ is (nearly) the same for every reading, and the graph of p against 1/V is a straight line through the origin. So ⟪p\\propto 1/V⟫: Boyle's law is verified."},{"type":"h","text":"2. Charles' law (constant pressure)"},{"type":"def","term":"Charles' law","text":"the volume of a fixed mass of gas is directly proportional to its absolute (kelvin) temperature, provided the pressure is kept constant."},{"type":"formula","tex":"V_{1}/T_{1}=V_{2}/T_{2}\\qquad (V/T=\\text{ constant})","where":["T MUST be in kelvin (K)."],"label":"CHARLES' LAW"},{"type":"p","text":"**Experiment to investigate Charles' law**"},{"type":"img","src":"img/week06/charles_apparatus","alt":"Figure 6.5 — Charles' law apparatus: dry air trapped in a capillary tube by a bead (index) of concentrated sulfuric acid","caption":"Figure 6.5 — Charles' law apparatus: dry air trapped in a capillary tube by a bead (index) of concentrated sulfuric acid","width":4.4,"w":1049,"h":662},{"type":"steps","items":["Dry air is trapped in a capillary tube (sealed at the bottom, open at the top) by a short index of concentrated sulfuric acid. The acid keeps the air **dry** and traps it. The tube is open to the air, so the pressure stays **constant** (atmospheric pressure + the small index).","The length of the air column is proportional to its volume (the tube has the same cross-section all along).","Heat the water bath slowly and **stir** so the air and water reach the same temperature. Wait before each reading.","Record the length of the air column and the temperature at intervals of about 10 °C.","Plot length (volume) against temperature in °C and extrapolate to find where the volume would be zero."]},{"type":"p","text":"**Results and conclusion:** a straight line which, extended backwards, cuts the temperature axis at about −273 °C. So ⟪V\\propto T⟫ (in kelvin)."},{"type":"h","text":"3. The pressure law (constant volume)"},{"type":"def","term":"Pressure law","text":"the pressure of a fixed mass of gas is directly proportional to its absolute (kelvin) temperature, provided the volume is kept constant."},{"type":"formula","tex":"p_{1}/T_{1}=p_{2}/T_{2}\\qquad (p/T=\\text{ constant})","where":["T MUST be in kelvin (K)."],"label":"PRESSURE LAW"},{"type":"img","src":"img/week06/pressure_apparatus","alt":"Figure 6.6 — Pressure law apparatus: a flask of dry air in a water bath, connected to a Bourdon gauge","caption":"Figure 6.6 — Pressure law apparatus: a flask of dry air in a water bath, connected to a Bourdon gauge","width":4.2,"w":840,"h":774},{"type":"steps","items":["A flask of dry air is placed in a water bath so that it is **completely covered** by the water. It is connected by a **short, narrow** tube to a pressure gauge (so almost all the air is at the water temperature).","Heat the water slowly and stir. At each temperature, wait for the gauge reading to steady, then record temperature and pressure.","Plot pressure against temperature (°C). Extrapolate the straight line to ⟪p=0⟫: it cuts the axis at about −273 °C.","Plotting p against T (in K) gives a straight line through the origin: ⟪p\\propto T⟫."]},{"type":"h","text":"4. The general gas law"},{"type":"p","text":"When pressure, volume AND temperature all change, combine the three laws into one:"},{"type":"formula","tex":"p_{1}V_{1}/T_{1}=p_{2}V_{2}/T_{2}\\qquad (pV/T=\\text{ constant})","where":["Use it for a FIXED MASS of gas. T in kelvin. p and V in any units, but the same on both sides.","If one quantity is constant, simply cross it out — you get back Boyle's, Charles' or the pressure law."],"label":"GENERAL GAS LAW"},{"type":"h","text":"Method for every gas-law problem"},{"type":"steps","items":["List the data: p~1~ = …, V~1~ = …, T~1~ = …;  p~2~ = …, V~2~ = …, T~2~ = …  (put ? for the unknown).","Convert every temperature to kelvin (+ 273).","Write the correct law (cross out anything that stays constant).","Substitute, then rearrange for the unknown.","Give the answer with a unit. If you want °C, subtract 273 at the very end.","Sense check: compressing → pressure up; heating → volume or pressure up."]},{"type":"warn","text":"The most common error is using °C. Example: heating a gas from 20 °C to 40 °C does NOT double its volume: ⟪(40+273)/(20+273)=313/293=1.07⟫, an increase of only 7%."}],"examples":[{"id":"week06.6.2.e1","title":"Boyle's law","question":"A fixed mass of air at constant temperature has a volume of 60 cm^3^ at a pressure of 100 kPa. It is compressed to 40 cm^3^. Calculate the new pressure.","steps":[{"label":"Data","text":"⟪p_{1}=100\\,\\mathrm{kPa}⟫, ⟪V_{1}=60\\,\\mathrm{cm}^{3}⟫, ⟪V_{2}=40\\,\\mathrm{cm}^{3}⟫, p~2~ = ?  (temperature constant → Boyle)"},{"label":"Formula","text":"⟪p_{1}V_{1}=p_{2}V_{2}⟫"},{"label":"Substitute","text":"⟪100\\times 60=p_{2}\\times 40⟫"},{"label":"Rearrange","text":"⟪p_{2}=6000\\div 40=150\\,\\mathrm{kPa}⟫"},{"label":"Check","text":"Volume went down, pressure went up ✓"}],"answer":"⟪p_{2}=150\\,\\mathrm{kPa}⟫"},{"id":"week06.6.2.e2","title":"Charles' law","question":"A balloon holds 300 cm^3^ of air at 27 °C. At constant pressure it is warmed to 127 °C. Find the new volume.","steps":[{"label":"Convert","text":"⟪T_{1}=27+273=300\\,\\mathrm{K}⟫;  ⟪T_{2}=127+273=400\\,\\mathrm{K}⟫"},{"label":"Formula","text":"⟪V_{1}/T_{1}=V_{2}/T_{2}\\rightarrow V_{2}=V_{1}\\times T_{2}/T_{1}⟫"},{"label":"Substitute","text":"⟪V_{2}=300\\times 400\\div 300⟫"},{"label":"Answer","text":"⟪V_{2}=400\\,\\mathrm{cm}^{3}⟫"}],"answer":"400 cm^3^"},{"id":"week06.6.2.e3","title":"Pressure law — a car tyre","question":"A tyre has air at 200 kPa at 27 °C. After a long drive the air is at 57 °C. Assuming the volume is constant, find the new pressure.","steps":[{"label":"Convert","text":"⟪T_{1}=300\\,\\mathrm{K}⟫;  ⟪T_{2}=57+273=330\\,\\mathrm{K}⟫"},{"label":"Formula","text":"⟪p_{1}/T_{1}=p_{2}/T_{2}\\rightarrow p_{2}=p_{1}\\times T_{2}/T_{1}⟫"},{"label":"Substitute","text":"⟪p_{2}=200\\times 330\\div 300⟫"},{"label":"Answer","text":"⟪p_{2}=220\\,\\mathrm{kPa}⟫"}],"answer":"220 kPa"},{"id":"week06.6.2.e4","title":"General gas law","question":"2.0 litres of gas at 100 kPa and 27 °C is changed to 150 kPa and 77 °C. Calculate its new volume.","steps":[{"label":"Convert","text":"⟪T_{1}=300\\,\\mathrm{K}⟫;  ⟪T_{2}=77+273=350\\,\\mathrm{K}⟫"},{"label":"Formula","text":"⟪p_{1}V_{1}/T_{1}=p_{2}V_{2}/T_{2}\\rightarrow V_{2}=p_{1}V_{1}T_{2}/\\ (T_{1}p_{2})⟫"},{"label":"Substitute","text":"⟪V_{2}=(100\\times 2.0\\times 350)\\div (300\\times 150)=70\\,000\\div 45\\,000⟫"},{"label":"Answer","text":"⟪V_{2}=1.56⟫ litres (3 s.f.)"}],"answer":"1.6 litres (1.56 L)"}]},"tryit":[{"id":"week06.6.2.p1","kind":"self","q":"State Boyle's law.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":3,"scheme":"Pressure of a fixed mass of gas is inversely proportional to its volume (1), provided temperature is constant (1).","points":[{"text":"Pressure of a fixed mass of gas is inversely proportional to its volume","marks":1},{"text":"provided temperature is constant","marks":1}],"section":"try","hint":{"text":"Look back at Example 1 (Boyle's law). Start like this: Data: ⟪p_{1}=100\\,\\mathrm{kPa}⟫, ⟪V_{1}=60\\,\\mathrm{cm}^{3}⟫, ⟪V_{2}=40\\,\\mathrm{cm}^{3}⟫, p~2~ = ?  (temperature constant → Boyle)","from":1}},{"id":"week06.6.2.p2","kind":"num","q":"Air at 80 kPa occupies 150 cm^3^. At constant temperature it is compressed to 50 cm^3^. Calculate the new pressure.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":3,"scheme":"⟪p_{2}=p_{1}V_{1}/V_{2}=80\\times 150\\div 50⟫ (1) ⟪=240\\,\\mathrm{kPa}⟫ (1).","points":[{"text":"⟪p_{2}=p_{1}V_{1}/V_{2}=80\\times 150\\div 50⟫","marks":1},{"text":"⟪=240\\,\\mathrm{kPa}⟫","marks":1}],"num":{"value":240,"sf":2,"unit":"kPa","unitTex":"\\mathrm{kPa}"},"section":"try","hint":{"text":"Look back at Example 1 (Boyle's law). Start like this: Data: ⟪p_{1}=100\\,\\mathrm{kPa}⟫, ⟪V_{1}=60\\,\\mathrm{cm}^{3}⟫, ⟪V_{2}=40\\,\\mathrm{cm}^{3}⟫, p~2~ = ?  (temperature constant → Boyle)","from":1}}],"practice":[{"id":"week06.6.2.p3","kind":"num","q":"A syringe holds 30 cm^3^ of gas at 200 kPa. The pressure is reduced to 120 kPa at constant temperature. Find the new volume.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":3,"scheme":"⟪V_{2}=p_{1}V_{1}/p_{2}=200\\times 30\\div 120⟫ (1) ⟪=50\\,\\mathrm{cm}^{3}⟫ (1).","points":[{"text":"⟪V_{2}=p_{1}V_{1}/p_{2}=200\\times 30\\div 120⟫","marks":1},{"text":"⟪=50\\,\\mathrm{cm}^{3}⟫","marks":1}],"num":{"value":50,"sf":1,"unit":"cm^3^","unitTex":"\\mathrm{cm}^{3}"},"section":"practice","hint":{"text":"Look back at Example 2 (Charles' law). Start like this: Convert: ⟪T_{1}=27+273=300\\,\\mathrm{K}⟫;  ⟪T_{2}=127+273=400\\,\\mathrm{K}⟫","from":2}},{"id":"week06.6.2.p4","kind":"num","q":"A gas occupies 500 cm^3^ at 7 °C. Find its volume at 77 °C if the pressure is constant.","marks":3,"level":2,"lines":4,"scheme":"⟪T_{1}=280\\,\\mathrm{K}⟫, ⟪T_{2}=350\\,\\mathrm{K}⟫ (1); ⟪V_{2}=500\\times 350\\div 280⟫ (1) ⟪=625\\,\\mathrm{cm}^{3}⟫ (1).","points":[{"text":"⟪T_{1}=280\\,\\mathrm{K}⟫, ⟪T_{2}=350\\,\\mathrm{K}⟫","marks":1},{"text":"⟪V_{2}=500\\times 350\\div 280⟫","marks":1},{"text":"⟪=625\\,\\mathrm{cm}^{3}⟫","marks":1}],"num":{"value":625,"sf":3,"unit":"cm^3^","unitTex":"\\mathrm{cm}^{3}"},"section":"practice","hint":{"text":"Look back at Example 2 (Charles' law). Start like this: Convert: ⟪T_{1}=27+273=300\\,\\mathrm{K}⟫;  ⟪T_{2}=127+273=400\\,\\mathrm{K}⟫","from":2}},{"id":"week06.6.2.p5","kind":"self","q":"A sealed gas cylinder is at 300 kPa at 27 °C. At what temperature (in °C) will its pressure reach 400 kPa?","marks":3,"level":2,"lines":4,"scheme":"⟪T_{1}=300\\,\\mathrm{K}⟫ (1); ⟪T_{2}=p_{2}T_{1}/p_{1}=400\\times 300\\div 300=400\\,\\mathrm{K}⟫ (1); ⟪=400-273=127\\,{}^{\\circ}\\mathrm{C}⟫ (1).","points":[{"text":"⟪T_{1}=300\\,\\mathrm{K}⟫","marks":1},{"text":"⟪T_{2}=p_{2}T_{1}/p_{1}=400\\times 300\\div 300=400\\,\\mathrm{K}⟫","marks":1},{"text":"⟪=400-273=127\\,{}^{\\circ}\\mathrm{C}⟫","marks":1}],"section":"practice","hint":{"text":"Look back at Example 1 (Boyle's law). Start like this: Data: ⟪p_{1}=100\\,\\mathrm{kPa}⟫, ⟪V_{1}=60\\,\\mathrm{cm}^{3}⟫, ⟪V_{2}=40\\,\\mathrm{cm}^{3}⟫, p~2~ = ?  (temperature constant → Boyle)","from":1}},{"id":"week06.6.2.p6","kind":"num","q":"120 cm^3^ of gas at 100 kPa and 27 °C is compressed to 90 cm^3^ and its temperature rises to 47 °C. Calculate the new pressure.","marks":3,"level":2,"lines":4,"scheme":"⟪T_{1}=300\\,\\mathrm{K}⟫, ⟪T_{2}=320\\,\\mathrm{K}⟫ (1); ⟪p_{2}=p_{1}V_{1}T_{2}/(T_{1}V_{2})=100\\times 120\\times 320\\div (300\\times 90)⟫ (1) ⟪=142\\,\\mathrm{kPa}⟫ (1).","points":[{"text":"⟪T_{1}=300\\,\\mathrm{K}⟫, ⟪T_{2}=320\\,\\mathrm{K}⟫","marks":1},{"text":"⟪p_{2}=p_{1}V_{1}T_{2}/(T_{1}V_{2})=100\\times 120\\times 320\\div (300\\times 90)⟫","marks":1},{"text":"⟪=142\\,\\mathrm{kPa}⟫","marks":1}],"num":{"value":142,"sf":3,"unit":"kPa","unitTex":"\\mathrm{kPa}"},"section":"practice","hint":{"text":"Look back at Example 1 (Boyle's law). Start like this: Data: ⟪p_{1}=100\\,\\mathrm{kPa}⟫, ⟪V_{1}=60\\,\\mathrm{cm}^{3}⟫, ⟪V_{2}=40\\,\\mathrm{cm}^{3}⟫, p~2~ = ?  (temperature constant → Boyle)","from":1}},{"id":"week06.6.2.p7","kind":"self","q":"In the Charles' law experiment, give TWO reasons for using a bead (index) of concentrated sulfuric acid.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":3,"scheme":"It traps the air (acts as a movable index / seal) (1); it is a drying agent so keeps the air dry (water vapour would condense/evaporate and spoil results) (1).","points":[{"text":"It traps the air (acts as a movable index / seal)","marks":1},{"text":"it is a drying agent so keeps the air dry (water vapour would condense/evaporate and spoil results)","marks":1}],"section":"practice"},{"id":"week06.6.2.p8","kind":"self","q":"State TWO precautions to take in the Boyle's law experiment.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":3,"scheme":"Wait after each change for the air to return to room temperature (keep temperature constant) (1); read the oil level at eye level to avoid parallax / tap the gauge / change pressure slowly (1).","points":[{"text":"Wait after each change for the air to return to room temperature (keep temperature constant)","marks":1},{"text":"read the oil level at eye level to avoid parallax / tap the gauge / change pressure slowly","marks":1}],"section":"practice"}],"exit":[{"id":"week06.6.2.p9","kind":"self","q":"A student obtains these readings for a fixed mass of gas at constant temperature: p (kPa): 100, 125, 160, 200;  V (cm^3^): 40, 32, 25, 20. Show that the results obey Boyle's law.","marks":3,"level":2,"lines":4,"scheme":"⟪pV=100\\times 40=4000⟫; ⟪125\\times 32=4000⟫; ⟪160\\times 25=4000⟫; 200 × 20 = 4000 (2: all four calculated; 1 if two or three) ; pV is constant so ⟪p\\propto 1/V⟫ — Boyle's law obeyed (1).","points":[{"text":"⟪pV=100\\times 40=4000⟫; ⟪125\\times 32=4000⟫; ⟪160\\times 25=4000⟫; ⟪200\\times 20=4000⟫","marks":2},{"text":"pV is constant so ⟪p\\propto 1/V⟫ — Boyle's law obeyed","marks":1}],"section":"exit"},{"id":"week06.6.2.p10","kind":"self","q":"A tyre is at 250 kPa at 22 °C in the morning. After driving, the air is at 47 °C. (i) Calculate the new pressure (volume constant). (ii) Why do drivers check tyre pressure when the tyres are cold?","marks":4,"level":3,"lines":5,"scheme":"(i) ⟪T_{1}=295\\,\\mathrm{K}⟫, ⟪T_{2}=320\\,\\mathrm{K}⟫ (1); ⟪p_{2}=250\\times 320\\div 295⟫ (1) ⟪=271\\,\\mathrm{kPa}⟫ (1). (ii) Hot tyres give a higher reading than the true cold pressure, so the tyre could be under-inflated if adjusted when hot (1).","points":[{"text":"(i) ⟪T_{1}=295\\,\\mathrm{K}⟫, ⟪T_{2}=320\\,\\mathrm{K}⟫","marks":1},{"text":"⟪p_{2}=250\\times 320\\div 295⟫","marks":1},{"text":"⟪=271\\,\\mathrm{kPa}⟫","marks":1},{"text":"(ii) Hot tyres give a higher reading than the true cold pressure, so the tyre could be under-inflated if adjusted when hot","marks":1}],"section":"exit"}],"warmup":[{"id":"week06.6.2.w1","kind":"num","q":"A liquid warms from 20 °C to 65 °C. What is the rise in temperature in kelvin?","marks":1,"level":1,"lines":1,"scheme":"45 K (a change is the same number on both scales) (1).","points":[{"text":"45 K (a change is the same number on both scales)","marks":1}],"num":{"value":45,"sf":2,"unit":"K","unitTex":"\\mathrm{K}"},"conf":"low","section":"warmup","src":"week06.6.1.p5"},{"id":"week06.6.2.w2","kind":"num","q":"Convert 25 °C to kelvin.","marks":1,"level":1,"lines":1,"scheme":"⟪25+273=298\\,\\mathrm{K}⟫ (1).","points":[{"text":"⟪25+273=298\\,\\mathrm{K}⟫","marks":1}],"num":{"value":298,"sf":3,"unit":"K","unitTex":"\\mathrm{K}"},"section":"warmup","src":"week06.6.1.p2"},{"id":"week06.6.2.w3","kind":"num","q":"Convert −40 °C to kelvin.","marks":1,"level":1,"lines":1,"scheme":"⟪-40+273=233\\,\\mathrm{K}⟫ (1).","points":[{"text":"⟪-40+273=233\\,\\mathrm{K}⟫","marks":1}],"num":{"value":233,"sf":3,"unit":"K","unitTex":"\\mathrm{K}"},"section":"warmup","src":"week06.6.1.p4"}],"n":39},{"id":"week06.6.3","kind":"unit","unit":"6.3","title":"Explaining the Gas Laws with Kinetic Theory","titleText":"Explaining the Gas Laws with Kinetic Theory","goals":[{"code":"B2.12","text":"Explain the gas laws using the kinetic theory of gases"}],"codes":["B2.12"],"words":[{"term":"Absolute zero","def":"The lowest possible temperature, 0 K or −273 °C; particles have minimum kinetic energy."},{"term":"Boyle's law","def":"⟪p\\propto 1/V⟫ for a fixed mass of gas at constant temperature."},{"term":"Charles' law","def":"⟪V\\propto T⟫ (kelvin) for a fixed mass of gas at constant pressure."},{"term":"Pressure law","def":"⟪p\\propto T⟫ (kelvin) for a fixed mass of gas at constant volume."}],"learn":{"notes":[{"type":"p","text":"The gas laws are **observations**. Kinetic theory **explains** them, using the idea that a gas is made of tiny particles in constant, random motion."},{"type":"h","text":"How a gas makes pressure"},{"type":"steps","items":["Gas particles move randomly at high speed.","They collide with the walls of the container and bounce off.","Each collision changes the particle's momentum, so it pushes on the wall with a small **force**.","Millions of collisions every second on every square metre give a steady force per unit area = **pressure**."]},{"type":"remember","text":"Pressure depends on (1) HOW OFTEN particles hit the walls (frequency of collisions) and (2) HOW HARD they hit (their speed / kinetic energy). Every kinetic-theory explanation uses these two ideas."},{"type":"h","text":"Boyle's law explained (temperature constant)"},{"type":"img","src":"img/week06/kinetic_boyle","alt":"Figure 6.7 — Halving the volume at constant temperature: the same particles hit the walls twice as often","caption":"Figure 6.7 — Halving the volume at constant temperature: the same particles hit the walls twice as often","width":5,"w":1108,"h":574},{"type":"bullets","items":["Temperature constant → average kinetic energy (speed) of the particles is unchanged, so each collision is just as hard.","Volume decreased → the same number of particles in a smaller space; they travel a shorter distance between walls.","So they hit each unit area of the walls **more often** (more collisions per second).","More force per unit area → **pressure increases**. Halve the volume → twice the collisions per second → double the pressure."]},{"type":"h","text":"Pressure law explained (volume constant)"},{"type":"bullets","items":["Temperature increased → particles gain kinetic energy and move **faster**.","They hit the walls **harder** (bigger change in momentum, bigger force each hit) AND **more often**.","Volume is fixed, so the larger force acts on the same area → **pressure increases**."]},{"type":"h","text":"Charles' law explained (pressure constant)"},{"type":"bullets","items":["Temperature increased → particles move faster and hit the walls harder and more often.","This would increase the pressure — but the pressure is kept constant (e.g. a free piston or the acid index can move).","So the gas **expands**: the volume increases until the particles hit each unit area less often, bringing the pressure back to its original value.","Result: higher temperature → larger volume."]},{"type":"h","text":"Absolute zero explained"},{"type":"p","text":"As a gas is cooled, its particles slow down. At absolute zero they would have their **minimum kinetic energy** — they could not slow down any more, so they would exert no pressure on the walls."},{"type":"tip","text":"Exam answers to \"Use the kinetic theory to explain…\" usually earn 3 marks: (1) what happens to the SPEED/KE of the particles, (2) what happens to the NUMBER of collisions per second (frequency) and/or the FORCE of each collision, (3) so the force per unit area (PRESSURE) changes. Always say \"per unit area\" and \"per second\"."},{"type":"warn","text":"Do NOT say the particles themselves get bigger when a gas is heated or expands. The particles stay the same size — the SPACES between them increase."},{"type":"table","headers":["Change","Particle speed","Collisions per second","Force per collision","Result"],"rows":[["Compress at constant T","same","more","same","p increases"],["Heat at constant V","faster","more","bigger","p increases"],["Heat at constant p","faster","fewer per unit area (because V grows)","bigger","V increases"],["Cool to absolute zero","minimum","none","none","p → 0"]]}],"examples":[{"id":"week06.6.3.e1","title":"Explain a pressure rise","question":"A sealed can of air is heated. Use the kinetic theory to explain why the pressure inside increases. [3 marks]","steps":[{"label":"","text":"Point 1: The particles gain kinetic energy and move faster. (1)"},{"label":"","text":"Point 2: They collide with the walls more often AND with more force (greater change of momentum). (1)"},{"label":"","text":"Point 3: The volume is constant, so the force per unit area on the walls — the pressure — increases. (1)"}],"answer":"Faster particles → more frequent, harder collisions → greater force per unit area = higher pressure."},{"id":"week06.6.3.e2","title":"Explain Boyle's law","question":"A bicycle pump is sealed and its piston pushed in slowly (temperature constant). Explain why the pressure rises.","steps":[{"label":"","text":"Temperature constant, so the particles' average speed (KE) is unchanged. (1)"},{"label":"","text":"The volume is smaller, so the particles hit each unit area of the walls more often (more collisions per second). (1)"},{"label":"","text":"Greater force per unit area → greater pressure. (1)"}],"answer":"Same speed, more frequent collisions on each unit area → higher pressure."}]},"tryit":[{"id":"week06.6.3.p1","kind":"self","q":"Explain, in terms of particles, how a gas exerts a pressure on the walls of its container.","marks":3,"level":2,"lines":4,"scheme":"Particles move randomly at high speed (1); they collide with the walls and change momentum, exerting a force (1); many collisions per second on each unit area give pressure (force per unit area) (1).","points":[{"text":"Particles move randomly at high speed","marks":1},{"text":"they collide with the walls and change momentum, exerting a force","marks":1},{"text":"many collisions per second on each unit area give pressure (force per unit area)","marks":1}],"section":"try","hint":{"text":"Look back at Example 1 (Explain a pressure rise). Start like this: Point 1: The particles gain kinetic energy and move faster. (1)","from":1}},{"id":"week06.6.3.p2","kind":"self","q":"State TWO ways in which the collisions of gas particles with a wall change when the gas is heated at constant volume.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":3,"scheme":"Collisions are more frequent (more per second) (1); each collision is harder / exerts a greater force (particles faster) (1).","points":[{"text":"Collisions are more frequent (more per second)","marks":1},{"text":"each collision is harder / exerts a greater force (particles faster)","marks":1}],"section":"try"}],"practice":[{"id":"week06.6.3.p3","kind":"self","q":"Use the kinetic theory to explain why the pressure of a gas increases when it is compressed at constant temperature.","marks":3,"level":2,"lines":4,"scheme":"Temperature constant so average speed/KE of particles unchanged (1); smaller volume → particles hit the walls more often/more collisions per unit area per second (1); greater force per unit area → higher pressure (1).","points":[{"text":"Temperature constant so average speed/KE of particles unchanged","marks":1},{"text":"smaller volume → particles hit the walls more often/more collisions per unit area per second","marks":1},{"text":"greater force per unit area → higher pressure","marks":1}],"section":"practice","hint":{"text":"Look back at Example 1 (Explain a pressure rise). Start like this: Point 1: The particles gain kinetic energy and move faster. (1)","from":1}},{"id":"week06.6.3.p4","kind":"self","q":"Explain why a gas expands when heated at constant pressure.","marks":3,"level":2,"lines":4,"scheme":"Particles move faster and hit walls harder and more often (1); this would increase pressure, but the pressure is kept constant / piston is free to move (1); so the gas expands until collisions per unit area per second fall and pressure returns to original value (1).","points":[{"text":"Particles move faster and hit walls harder and more often","marks":1},{"text":"this would increase pressure, but the pressure is kept constant / piston is free to move","marks":1},{"text":"so the gas expands until collisions per unit area per second fall and pressure returns to original value","marks":1}],"section":"practice","hint":{"text":"Look back at Example 1 (Explain a pressure rise). Start like this: Point 1: The particles gain kinetic energy and move faster. (1)","from":1}},{"id":"week06.6.3.p5","kind":"self","q":"A student writes: \"When a gas is heated, its particles get bigger, so it expands.\" Correct this statement.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":3,"scheme":"The particles do not get bigger (1); they move faster and the spaces between them increase (1).","points":[{"text":"The particles do not get bigger","marks":1},{"text":"they move faster and the spaces between them increase","marks":1}],"section":"practice"}],"exit":[{"id":"week06.6.3.p6","kind":"self","q":"Explain why an aerosol can must not be thrown into a fire.","marks":3,"level":2,"lines":4,"scheme":"Gas inside is heated; particles move faster (1); more frequent/harder collisions so pressure rises greatly (volume fixed) (1); the can may explode/burst (1).","points":[{"text":"Gas inside is heated; particles move faster","marks":1},{"text":"more frequent/harder collisions so pressure rises greatly (volume fixed)","marks":1},{"text":"the can may explode/burst","marks":1}],"section":"exit"},{"id":"week06.6.3.p7","kind":"self","q":"Use the kinetic theory to explain what is meant by absolute zero.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":3,"scheme":"The temperature at which particles have their minimum (least possible) kinetic energy (1); so they would exert no pressure / gas pressure would be zero (1).","points":[{"text":"The temperature at which particles have their minimum (least possible) kinetic energy","marks":1},{"text":"so they would exert no pressure / gas pressure would be zero","marks":1}],"section":"exit"}],"warmup":[{"id":"week06.6.3.w1","kind":"num","q":"Air at 80 kPa occupies 150 cm^3^. At constant temperature it is compressed to 50 cm^3^. Calculate the new pressure.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":3,"scheme":"⟪p_{2}=p_{1}V_{1}/V_{2}=80\\times 150\\div 50⟫ (1) ⟪=240\\,\\mathrm{kPa}⟫ (1).","points":[{"text":"⟪p_{2}=p_{1}V_{1}/V_{2}=80\\times 150\\div 50⟫","marks":1},{"text":"⟪=240\\,\\mathrm{kPa}⟫","marks":1}],"num":{"value":240,"sf":2,"unit":"kPa","unitTex":"\\mathrm{kPa}"},"section":"warmup","src":"week06.6.2.p2"},{"id":"week06.6.3.w2","kind":"self","q":"State Boyle's law.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":3,"scheme":"Pressure of a fixed mass of gas is inversely proportional to its volume (1), provided temperature is constant (1).","points":[{"text":"Pressure of a fixed mass of gas is inversely proportional to its volume","marks":1},{"text":"provided temperature is constant","marks":1}],"section":"warmup","src":"week06.6.2.p1"},{"id":"week06.6.3.w3","kind":"num","q":"A syringe holds 30 cm^3^ of gas at 200 kPa. The pressure is reduced to 120 kPa at constant temperature. Find the new volume.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":3,"scheme":"⟪V_{2}=p_{1}V_{1}/p_{2}=200\\times 30\\div 120⟫ (1) ⟪=50\\,\\mathrm{cm}^{3}⟫ (1).","points":[{"text":"⟪V_{2}=p_{1}V_{1}/p_{2}=200\\times 30\\div 120⟫","marks":1},{"text":"⟪=50\\,\\mathrm{cm}^{3}⟫","marks":1}],"num":{"value":50,"sf":1,"unit":"cm^3^","unitTex":"\\mathrm{cm}^{3}"},"section":"warmup","src":"week06.6.2.p3"}],"n":40},{"id":"week06.6.4","kind":"unit","unit":"6.4","title":"Conduction and Convection","titleText":"Conduction and Convection","goals":[{"code":"B4.1","text":"Explain the transfer of thermal energy by conduction"},{"code":"B4.2","text":"Explain the transfer of thermal energy by convection"}],"codes":["B4.1","B4.2"],"words":[{"term":"Conduction","def":"Heat transfer through a material by particle vibrations (and free electrons), without the material moving."},{"term":"Convection","def":"Heat transfer in a fluid by movement of the fluid itself, due to density differences."},{"term":"Convection current","def":"Circulating flow: hot, less dense fluid rises; cool, denser fluid sinks."},{"term":"Radiation","def":"Heat transfer by infrared electromagnetic waves; needs no medium."}],"learn":{"notes":[{"type":"p","text":"Thermal energy (heat) always flows from a **hotter** place to a **colder** place. It can travel in three ways: **conduction**, **convection** and **radiation**. This unit covers the first two, which both need a material (a medium)."},{"type":"table","headers":["Method","Needs a medium?","Mainly happens in","How"],"rows":[["Conduction","yes","solids (especially metals)","vibrating particles pass energy to neighbours; free electrons in metals"],["Convection","yes","fluids (liquids and gases)","the warm fluid itself moves (rises), carrying energy"],["Radiation","NO — works in a vacuum","anywhere; through empty space","infrared electromagnetic waves"]]},{"type":"h","text":"Conduction (B4.1)"},{"type":"def","term":"Conduction","text":"the transfer of thermal energy through a material from particle to particle, from the hotter part to the colder part, WITHOUT the material itself moving."},{"type":"p","text":"**How it works:**"},{"type":"bullets","items":["At the hot end, particles gain energy and **vibrate more** about their fixed positions.","They collide with neighbouring particles and pass on some of this energy. Energy is passed along the material, particle by particle. This is slow.","In **metals** there are also **free (delocalised) electrons**. They gain kinetic energy at the hot end and move quickly through the whole metal, carrying energy to the cold end. This is why metals are the best conductors."]},{"type":"table","headers":["Good conductors","Poor conductors (insulators)"],"rows":[["copper, aluminium, iron, steel (all metals)","wood, plastic, glass, rubber, cloth, wool"],["(silver is the best)","water and all liquids (except mercury)"],["","air and all gases — the best insulators, especially when TRAPPED so it cannot convect (e.g. in wool, feathers, foam, fibreglass)"]]},{"type":"p","text":"**Experiment: comparing conductors**"},{"type":"img","src":"img/week06/conduction_rods","alt":"Figure 6.8 — Rods of equal length and thickness, with pins fixed by wax, heated at one end by hot water","caption":"Figure 6.8 — Rods of equal length and thickness, with pins fixed by wax, heated at one end by hot water","width":5.2,"w":1134,"h":550},{"type":"steps","items":["Take rods of different materials but the **same length and diameter**. Coat each with an even layer of wax (or fix pins along it with wax).","Put one end of every rod into the **same** hot water tank at the same time.","Watch how far along each rod the wax melts (or how many pins drop off) after a fixed time."]},{"type":"p","text":"**Result:** the wax melts furthest along copper, then aluminium, then iron; glass hardly at all. **Conclusion:** copper is the best conductor of these; glass is a poor conductor (insulator)."},{"type":"p","text":"**Experiment: water is a poor conductor**"},{"type":"img","src":"img/week06/ice_tube","alt":"Figure 6.9 — Ice weighted down at the bottom of a tube of water; the water at the top is boiled","caption":"Figure 6.9 — Ice weighted down at the bottom of a tube of water; the water at the top is boiled","width":4.4,"w":1035,"h":603},{"type":"p","text":"Ice is held at the bottom of a test tube of water by a metal gauze weight. The tube is heated near the **top**. The water at the top boils, but the ice at the bottom does **not** melt for a long time. Heat cannot travel down by convection (warm water stays at the top), and water conducts very poorly. **Conclusion: water is a poor conductor of heat.**"},{"type":"tip","text":"Why does a metal bench feel colder than a wooden bench in the same room? Both are at the SAME temperature. Metal is a good conductor, so it conducts heat away from your hand quickly; your hand loses heat faster and feels cold. Never say \"the metal is colder\"."},{"type":"h","text":"Convection (B4.2)"},{"type":"def","term":"Convection","text":"the transfer of thermal energy in a fluid (liquid or gas) by the movement of the fluid itself, caused by differences in density. Hot, less dense fluid rises; cooler, denser fluid sinks to take its place."},{"type":"p","text":"**How a convection current forms:**"},{"type":"steps","items":["The fluid near the heat source is heated. Its particles move faster and spread out, so the fluid **expands**.","Same mass in a bigger volume → its **density decreases**.","The less dense warm fluid **rises** (it floats up on the denser fluid around it).","Cooler, denser fluid **sinks** to take its place, and is then heated in turn.","This circulation is a **convection current**. It carries energy through the whole fluid."]},{"type":"img","src":"img/week06/convection_beaker","alt":"Figure 6.10 — A convection current shown by a crystal of potassium permanganate in water heated at one side","caption":"Figure 6.10 — A convection current shown by a crystal of potassium permanganate in water heated at one side","width":4,"w":870,"h":565},{"type":"p","text":"**Experiment:** drop one crystal of potassium permanganate (purple) to the bottom of a beaker of water, at one side. Heat gently under the crystal only. **Observation:** a purple stream rises above the crystal, moves across the top, sinks down the far side and returns along the bottom — a loop. **Conclusion:** heated water rises and cool water sinks, forming a convection current. (For air, use smoke from a smouldering paper over a candle in a box with two chimneys.)"},{"type":"warn","text":"Never write \"heat rises\". Heat is energy, not a substance. Write \"HOT AIR (or hot water) rises because it is LESS DENSE\". Also: convection CANNOT happen in solids, because the particles are not free to move from place to place."},{"type":"h","text":"Convection in everyday life"},{"type":"img","src":"img/week06/sea_breeze","alt":"Figure 6.11 — (a) Sea breeze by day; (b) land breeze by night","caption":"Figure 6.11 — (a) Sea breeze by day; (b) land breeze by night","width":5.6,"w":1294,"h":534},{"type":"bullets","items":["**Sea breeze (day):** land heats up faster than the sea (land has a lower specific heat capacity). Air over the land is warmed, becomes less dense and rises. Cooler, denser air from over the sea moves in to replace it — a cool breeze blows from SEA to LAND.","**Land breeze (night):** land cools faster than the sea. Now the air over the (warmer) sea rises and air flows from LAND to SEA. Caribbean fishermen traditionally sail out at night on the land breeze and return by day on the sea breeze.","**Heating element at the BOTTOM** of a kettle or water heater: hot water rises and the whole volume circulates.","**Freezing compartment / cooling unit at the TOP** of a refrigerator: cold, dense air sinks and cools everything below.","**Air conditioners mounted high** on a wall: cold air sinks through the room.","**Ventilation:** hot, stale air rises and escapes through high vents/louvres; cool air enters lower down."]}],"examples":[{"id":"week06.6.4.e1","title":"Explaining convection","question":"Explain why the heating element in an electric kettle is placed at the bottom. [3 marks]","steps":[{"label":"","text":"Water near the element is heated, expands and becomes less dense. (1)"},{"label":"","text":"The less dense hot water rises; cooler, denser water sinks to replace it. (1)"},{"label":"","text":"A convection current forms, so ALL the water is heated (an element at the top would heat only the top layer). (1)"}],"answer":"Bottom element → convection current circulates and heats the whole kettle."},{"id":"week06.6.4.e2","title":"Metal feels colder","question":"A tile floor and a carpet in the same room are both at 25 °C. Explain why the tiles feel colder to bare feet.","steps":[{"label":"","text":"Both are at the same temperature (thermal equilibrium with the room)."},{"label":"","text":"Tile is a better conductor than carpet (carpet traps air, a poor conductor)."},{"label":"","text":"So heat is conducted away from the feet faster by the tile — the feet lose energy faster and feel cold."}],"answer":"Tiles conduct heat away from the feet faster; they are NOT at a lower temperature."}]},"tryit":[{"id":"week06.6.4.p1","kind":"self","q":"Define **conduction**.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":2,"scheme":"Transfer of thermal energy through a material from hotter to colder region (1) by particles passing on vibrations / without the material moving (1).","points":[{"text":"Transfer of thermal energy through a material from hotter to colder region","marks":1},{"text":"by particles passing on vibrations / without the material moving","marks":1}],"section":"try"},{"id":"week06.6.4.p2","kind":"self","q":"Explain why metals are better conductors of heat than non-metals.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":3,"scheme":"Metals have free (delocalised) electrons (1) that gain KE and move quickly through the metal carrying energy (in addition to vibrations) (1).","points":[{"text":"Metals have free (delocalised) electrons","marks":1},{"text":"that gain KE and move quickly through the metal carrying energy (in addition to vibrations)","marks":1}],"section":"try"}],"practice":[{"id":"week06.6.4.p3","kind":"self","q":"Why are saucepan handles made of wood or plastic?","marks":1,"level":1,"lines":2,"scheme":"They are poor conductors (insulators), so heat is not conducted to the hand (1).","points":[{"text":"They are poor conductors (insulators), so heat is not conducted to the hand","marks":1}],"section":"practice"},{"id":"week06.6.4.p4","kind":"self","q":"State TWO conditions that must be kept the same when comparing the conductivity of rods of different metals.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":2,"scheme":"Any two: same length; same diameter/thickness; same starting temperature/same heat source; same amount of wax/same time (1 each).","points":[{"text":"Any two: same length; same diameter/thickness; same starting temperature/same heat source; same amount of wax/same time (1 each).","marks":2,"each":true}],"section":"practice"},{"id":"week06.6.4.p5","kind":"self","q":"Describe an experiment to show that water is a poor conductor of heat. Include what you would observe.","marks":4,"level":3,"lines":6,"scheme":"Ice weighed down (with gauze/metal) at bottom of test tube of water (1); heat the tube near the top (1); water at top boils (1); ice at bottom does not melt → water is a poor conductor (convection does not carry heat downward) (1).","points":[{"text":"Ice weighed down (with gauze/metal) at bottom of test tube of water","marks":1},{"text":"heat the tube near the top","marks":1},{"text":"water at top boils","marks":1},{"text":"ice at bottom does not melt → water is a poor conductor (convection does not carry heat downward)","marks":1}],"section":"practice"},{"id":"week06.6.4.p6","kind":"self","q":"Define **convection** and explain why it cannot occur in solids.","marks":3,"level":2,"lines":4,"scheme":"Transfer of heat in a fluid by movement of the fluid itself (1) due to density differences (hot, less dense fluid rises) (1). In solids the particles are fixed in position and cannot flow (1).","points":[{"text":"Transfer of heat in a fluid by movement of the fluid itself","marks":1},{"text":"due to density differences (hot, less dense fluid rises)","marks":1},{"text":"In solids the particles are fixed in position and cannot flow","marks":1}],"section":"practice","hint":{"text":"Look back at Example 1 (Explaining convection). Start like this: Water near the element is heated, expands and becomes less dense. (1)","from":1}},{"id":"week06.6.4.p7","kind":"self","q":"Explain how a sea breeze forms during the day.","marks":4,"level":3,"lines":5,"scheme":"Land heats up faster than sea (lower specific heat capacity) (1); air above land is heated, expands, becomes less dense (1) and rises (1); cooler denser air from over the sea moves in to replace it — breeze from sea to land (1).","points":[{"text":"Land heats up faster than sea (lower specific heat capacity)","marks":1},{"text":"air above land is heated, expands, becomes less dense","marks":1},{"text":"and rises","marks":1},{"text":"cooler denser air from over the sea moves in to replace it — breeze from sea to land","marks":1}],"section":"practice","hint":{"text":"Look back at Example 1 (Explaining convection). Start like this: Water near the element is heated, expands and becomes less dense. (1)","from":1}}],"exit":[{"id":"week06.6.4.p8","kind":"self","q":"Why is the freezer compartment placed at the top of a refrigerator?","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":3,"scheme":"Air cooled by the freezer becomes denser and sinks (1), setting up a convection current that cools the whole fridge (1).","points":[{"text":"Air cooled by the freezer becomes denser and sinks","marks":1},{"text":"setting up a convection current that cools the whole fridge","marks":1}],"section":"exit"},{"id":"week06.6.4.p9","kind":"self","q":"Explain why a woollen sweater keeps you warm.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":3,"scheme":"Wool traps air (1); trapped air is a poor conductor and cannot form convection currents, so heat loss from the body is reduced (1).","points":[{"text":"Wool traps air","marks":1},{"text":"trapped air is a poor conductor and cannot form convection currents, so heat loss from the body is reduced","marks":1}],"section":"exit"}],"warmup":[{"id":"week06.6.4.w1","kind":"self","q":"State TWO ways in which the collisions of gas particles with a wall change when the gas is heated at constant volume.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":3,"scheme":"Collisions are more frequent (more per second) (1); each collision is harder / exerts a greater force (particles faster) (1).","points":[{"text":"Collisions are more frequent (more per second)","marks":1},{"text":"each collision is harder / exerts a greater force (particles faster)","marks":1}],"section":"warmup","src":"week06.6.3.p2"},{"id":"week06.6.4.w2","kind":"self","q":"A student writes: \"When a gas is heated, its particles get bigger, so it expands.\" Correct this statement.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":3,"scheme":"The particles do not get bigger (1); they move faster and the spaces between them increase (1).","points":[{"text":"The particles do not get bigger","marks":1},{"text":"they move faster and the spaces between them increase","marks":1}],"section":"warmup","src":"week06.6.3.p5"},{"id":"week06.6.4.w3","kind":"self","q":"Use the kinetic theory to explain what is meant by absolute zero.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":3,"scheme":"The temperature at which particles have their minimum (least possible) kinetic energy (1); so they would exert no pressure / gas pressure would be zero (1).","points":[{"text":"The temperature at which particles have their minimum (least possible) kinetic energy","marks":1},{"text":"so they would exert no pressure / gas pressure would be zero","marks":1}],"section":"warmup","src":"week06.6.3.p7"}],"n":41},{"id":"week06.6.5","kind":"unit","unit":"6.5","title":"Radiation: Good and Bad Absorbers and Emitters","titleText":"Radiation: Good and Bad Absorbers and Emitters","goals":[{"code":"B4.3","text":"Explain the transfer of thermal energy by radiation"},{"code":"B4.4","text":"Conduct experiments to investigate the factors on which the absorption and emission of radiation depend"},{"code":"B4.5","text":"Recall that good absorbers of radiation are also good emitters"}],"codes":["B4.3","B4.4","B4.5"],"words":[{"term":"Conduction","def":"Heat transfer through a material by particle vibrations (and free electrons), without the material moving."},{"term":"Convection","def":"Heat transfer in a fluid by movement of the fluid itself, due to density differences."},{"term":"Radiation","def":"Heat transfer by infrared electromagnetic waves; needs no medium."},{"term":"Leslie cube","def":"Metal cube with different surfaces, used to compare emission of radiation."},{"term":"Thermopile","def":"A detector of infrared radiation, connected to a galvanometer."}],"learn":{"notes":[{"type":"h","text":"Radiation (B4.3)"},{"type":"def","term":"Radiation (thermal)","text":"the transfer of thermal energy by electromagnetic waves (mainly infrared). It needs NO medium and can travel through a vacuum at the speed of light ⟪(3.0\\times 10^{8}\\,\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-1})⟫."},{"type":"bullets","items":["ALL objects emit infrared radiation. The hotter an object, the more radiation it emits each second.","The Sun's energy reaches Earth through 150 million km of empty space — this can ONLY be by radiation (conduction and convection need particles).","Radiation travels in straight lines and can be reflected (by shiny surfaces) or absorbed (by dull, dark surfaces).","When radiation is absorbed, the surface gets hotter."]},{"type":"h","text":"What affects absorption and emission?"},{"type":"table","headers":["Surface","Absorbing (taking in)","Emitting (giving out)","Reflecting"],"rows":[["Dull (matt) black","BEST absorber","BEST emitter","worst reflector"],["Dark, rough","good","good","poor"],["White","poor","poor","good"],["Shiny silver (polished)","WORST absorber","WORST emitter","BEST reflector"]]},{"type":"p","text":"Other factors: the rate of emission also increases with **temperature** of the surface (hotter emits more) and **surface area** (bigger area emits more)."},{"type":"def","term":"Good absorbers are good emitters (B4.5)","text":"a surface that is a good absorber of radiation is also a good emitter of radiation; a poor absorber (good reflector) is also a poor emitter."},{"type":"h","text":"Experiment 1: comparing EMITTERS — the Leslie cube (B4.4)"},{"type":"img","src":"img/week06/leslie_cube","alt":"Figure 6.12 — Leslie cube: a metal cube with four different faces, filled with very hot water. A thermopile detects the radiation from each face.","caption":"Figure 6.12 — Leslie cube: a metal cube with four different faces, filled with very hot water. A thermopile detects the radiation from each face.","width":5,"w":1106,"h":654},{"type":"steps","items":["A Leslie cube is a hollow metal cube whose four sides have different surfaces: dull black, shiny silver, white, and dull grey/rough.","Fill it with boiling water so all faces are at the **same temperature**.","Place a radiation detector (thermopile connected to a galvanometer, or an infrared thermometer) at the **same distance** from each face in turn.","Record the reading for each face. (Simple alternative: hold the back of your hand near each face.)"]},{"type":"p","text":"**Result:** the dull black face gives the largest reading; the shiny silver face gives the smallest. **Conclusion:** dull black surfaces are the best emitters, shiny surfaces the worst."},{"type":"h","text":"Experiment 2: comparing ABSORBERS"},{"type":"img","src":"img/week06/absorb_plates","alt":"Figure 6.13 — Two metal plates, one dull black and one shiny, at equal distances from a radiant heater. A coin is fixed to the back of each with wax.","caption":"Figure 6.13 — Two metal plates, one dull black and one shiny, at equal distances from a radiant heater. A coin is fixed to the back of each with wax.","width":4.8,"w":1028,"h":614},{"type":"steps","items":["Two identical metal plates: one painted dull black, one polished shiny. Fix a coin (or cork) to the back of each with the same amount of wax.","Place an electric radiant heater **exactly midway** between them, so each receives the same radiation.","Switch on and observe which coin falls first."]},{"type":"p","text":"**Result:** the wax on the dull black plate melts first and its coin falls first. **Conclusion:** dull black surfaces are better absorbers than shiny surfaces."},{"type":"p","text":"**Experiment 3: cooling cans** — two identical cans, one painted dull black and one shiny, each filled with the same volume of hot water at the same starting temperature, with lids. Record the temperature every minute. The black can cools faster (it is the better emitter). Plotting temperature against time gives two cooling curves; the steeper curve belongs to the better emitter."},{"type":"img","src":"img/week06/cooling_cans","alt":"Figure 6.14 — Cooling curves for two cans with different surfaces","caption":"Figure 6.14 — Cooling curves for two cans with different surfaces","width":4.4,"w":1014,"h":634},{"type":"tip","text":"In a \"fair test\" question about radiation, the controlled variables are: same starting temperature, same volume/mass of water, same size and material of can, lid on both (to stop evaporation/convection), same room/surroundings, same distance from heater/detector."},{"type":"warn","text":"Do not confuse colour with temperature: a black object is not automatically hotter. It ABSORBS radiation faster in sunlight and LOSES heat faster by radiation when it is hotter than its surroundings."}],"examples":[{"id":"week06.6.5.e1","title":"Reading cooling curves","question":"Figure 6.14 shows cooling curves for a dull black can and a shiny can that both started at 80 °C. (a) Which curve is the black can? (b) Use the graph to find the temperature drop of each can in the first 10 minutes.","img":{"src":"img/week06/cooling_cans","alt":"Figure 6.14 shows cooling curves for a dull black can and a shiny can that both started at 80 °C. (a) Which curve is the black can? (b) Use the graph to find the temperature drop of each can in the first 10 minutes.","w":1014,"h":634},"steps":[{"label":"(a)","text":"Can X cools faster (steeper curve). Dull black is the best emitter, so **X is the dull black can**."},{"label":"(b) Can X","text":"At 10 min, ⟪X\\approx 43\\,{}^{\\circ}\\mathrm{C}⟫. Drop ⟪=80-43=37\\,{}^{\\circ}\\mathrm{C}⟫"},{"label":"(b) Can Y","text":"At 10 min, ⟪Y\\approx 58\\,{}^{\\circ}\\mathrm{C}⟫. Drop ⟪=80-58=22\\,{}^{\\circ}\\mathrm{C}⟫"}],"answer":"X is black; drops ≈ 37 °C (X) and ≈ 22 °C (Y)"},{"id":"week06.6.5.e2","title":"Explain in terms of absorbers","question":"Explain why houses in the Caribbean are often painted white and have shiny roofs.","steps":[{"label":"","text":"White and shiny surfaces are poor absorbers (good reflectors) of radiation."},{"label":"","text":"They reflect most of the Sun's infrared radiation, so less energy is absorbed."},{"label":"","text":"The inside of the house stays cooler."}],"answer":"Light, shiny surfaces reflect radiation → less absorbed → cooler house."}]},"tryit":[{"id":"week06.6.5.p1","kind":"self","q":"State TWO differences between radiation and conduction.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":3,"scheme":"Radiation needs no medium/can pass through a vacuum; conduction needs a material (1). Radiation is by electromagnetic (infrared) waves; conduction by particle vibrations/free electrons (1). (Also: radiation travels at speed of light.)","points":[{"text":"Radiation needs no medium/can pass through a vacuum; conduction needs a material","marks":1},{"text":"Radiation is by electromagnetic (infrared) waves; conduction by particle vibrations/free electrons (Also: radiation travels at speed of light.)","marks":1}],"section":"try"},{"id":"week06.6.5.p2","kind":"self","q":"How does energy from the Sun reach the Earth? Explain why it cannot be by conduction or convection.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":3,"scheme":"By radiation (1); space between Sun and Earth is a vacuum — no particles for conduction or convection (1).","points":[{"text":"By radiation","marks":1},{"text":"space between Sun and Earth is a vacuum — no particles for conduction or convection","marks":1}],"section":"try"}],"practice":[{"id":"week06.6.5.p3","kind":"self","q":"Which type of surface is (i) the best emitter, (ii) the best reflector of radiation?","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":2,"scheme":"(i) dull/matt black (1); (ii) shiny/polished silver (white) (1).","points":[{"text":"(i) dull/matt black","marks":1},{"text":"(ii) shiny/polished silver (white)","marks":1}],"section":"practice"},{"id":"week06.6.5.p4","kind":"self","q":"Describe an experiment using a Leslie cube to compare the emission of radiation from different surfaces.","marks":5,"level":3,"lines":7,"scheme":"Cube with faces of different surfaces (dull black, shiny, white…) (1); filled with hot/boiling water so all faces at same temperature (1); detector (thermopile + galvanometer / IR thermometer) placed same distance from each face (1); record readings (1); dull black gives highest reading — best emitter; shiny lowest (1).","points":[{"text":"Cube with faces of different surfaces (dull black, shiny, white…)","marks":1},{"text":"filled with hot/boiling water so all faces at same temperature","marks":1},{"text":"detector (thermopile + galvanometer / IR thermometer) placed same distance from each face","marks":1},{"text":"record readings","marks":1},{"text":"dull black gives highest reading — best emitter; shiny lowest","marks":1}],"section":"practice"},{"id":"week06.6.5.p5","kind":"self","q":"In the absorption experiment (Figure 6.13), why must the heater be placed exactly midway between the plates?","marks":1,"level":1,"lines":2,"scheme":"So both plates receive the same amount of radiation (fair test) (1).","points":[{"text":"So both plates receive the same amount of radiation (fair test)","marks":1}],"section":"practice"},{"id":"week06.6.5.p6","kind":"self","q":"State the relationship between how well a surface absorbs radiation and how well it emits radiation.","marks":1,"level":1,"lines":2,"scheme":"Good absorbers are good emitters (poor absorbers are poor emitters) (1).","points":[{"text":"Good absorbers are good emitters (poor absorbers are poor emitters)","marks":1}],"section":"practice"},{"id":"week06.6.5.p7","kind":"self","q":"Two identical cans, one dull black and one shiny, each contain 200 cm^3^ of water at 80 °C. (i) Which cools faster and why? (ii) State TWO variables that must be controlled.","marks":4,"level":3,"lines":5,"scheme":"(i) Dull black (1); it is a better emitter of radiation (1). (ii) Any two: same volume of water; same starting temperature; same size/material of can; lids on both; same surroundings (1 each).","points":[{"text":"(i) Dull black","marks":1},{"text":"it is a better emitter of radiation (ii) Any two: same volume of water; same starting temperature; same size/material of can; lids on both; same surroundings (1 each)","marks":1}],"conf":"low","section":"practice","hint":{"text":"Look back at Example 1 (Reading cooling curves). Start like this: (a): Can X cools faster (steeper curve). Dull black is the best emitter, so **X is the dull black can**.","from":1}}],"exit":[{"id":"week06.6.5.p8","kind":"self","q":"A cold drink is left in the sun in two identical bottles, one wrapped in black cloth and one in aluminium foil. Which drink warms more slowly? Explain.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":3,"scheme":"The foil-wrapped drink (1); shiny foil is a poor absorber / good reflector of radiation (1).","points":[{"text":"The foil-wrapped drink","marks":1},{"text":"shiny foil is a poor absorber / good reflector of radiation","marks":1}],"section":"exit"},{"id":"week06.6.5.p9","kind":"self","q":"Why are the cooling fins on the back of a refrigerator painted black?","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":3,"scheme":"Black is a good emitter of radiation (1), so heat is lost from the fins to the surroundings faster (1).","points":[{"text":"Black is a good emitter of radiation","marks":1},{"text":"so heat is lost from the fins to the surroundings faster","marks":1}],"section":"exit"}],"warmup":[{"id":"week06.6.5.w1","kind":"self","q":"Define **conduction**.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":2,"scheme":"Transfer of thermal energy through a material from hotter to colder region (1) by particles passing on vibrations / without the material moving (1).","points":[{"text":"Transfer of thermal energy through a material from hotter to colder region","marks":1},{"text":"by particles passing on vibrations / without the material moving","marks":1}],"section":"warmup","src":"week06.6.4.p1"},{"id":"week06.6.5.w2","kind":"self","q":"Why are saucepan handles made of wood or plastic?","marks":1,"level":1,"lines":2,"scheme":"They are poor conductors (insulators), so heat is not conducted to the hand (1).","points":[{"text":"They are poor conductors (insulators), so heat is not conducted to the hand","marks":1}],"section":"warmup","src":"week06.6.4.p3"},{"id":"week06.6.5.w3","kind":"self","q":"Explain why metals are better conductors of heat than non-metals.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":3,"scheme":"Metals have free (delocalised) electrons (1) that gain KE and move quickly through the metal carrying energy (in addition to vibrations) (1).","points":[{"text":"Metals have free (delocalised) electrons","marks":1},{"text":"that gain KE and move quickly through the metal carrying energy (in addition to vibrations)","marks":1}],"section":"warmup","src":"week06.6.4.p2"}],"n":42},{"id":"week06.6.6","kind":"unit","unit":"6.6","title":"Heat Transfer in Everyday Life","titleText":"Heat Transfer in Everyday Life","goals":[{"code":"B4.6","text":"Relate heat transfer to everyday applications (vacuum flask, solar water heater, greenhouse, cooking utensils)"}],"codes":["B4.6"],"words":[{"term":"Conduction","def":"Heat transfer through a material by particle vibrations (and free electrons), without the material moving."},{"term":"Convection","def":"Heat transfer in a fluid by movement of the fluid itself, due to density differences."},{"term":"Convection current","def":"Circulating flow: hot, less dense fluid rises; cool, denser fluid sinks."},{"term":"Radiation","def":"Heat transfer by infrared electromagnetic waves; needs no medium."},{"term":"Greenhouse effect","def":"Trapping of long-wavelength infrared by glass or atmospheric gases, warming the space beneath."},{"term":"Thermosiphon","def":"Circulation of water by convection (no pump), as in a solar water heater."}],"learn":{"notes":[{"type":"p","text":"In every application question, name the METHOD (conduction, convection or radiation), say what the feature DOES, and explain WHY in one physics sentence."},{"type":"h","text":"1. The vacuum flask (Thermos)"},{"type":"img","src":"img/week06/vacuum_flask","alt":"Figure 6.15 — Cross-section of a vacuum flask","caption":"Figure 6.15 — Cross-section of a vacuum flask","width":4.6,"w":971,"h":738},{"type":"table","headers":["Feature","Reduces","How"],"rows":[["Vacuum between the double glass walls","conduction AND convection","both need particles; a vacuum has none"],["Silvered (shiny) inner surfaces of the walls","radiation","shiny surfaces are poor emitters and reflect radiation back (towards a hot drink, or away from a cold one)"],["Stopper of cork/plastic","conduction, convection and evaporation","poor conductor; stops hot air/vapour escaping from the top"],["Insulating supports (cork/felt pads)","conduction","poor conductors hold the fragile glass vessel"],["Outer case","—","protects the fragile glass vessel"]]},{"type":"remember","text":"A vacuum flask keeps hot things HOT and cold things COLD — it simply slows down heat transfer in BOTH directions."},{"type":"h","text":"2. The solar water heater"},{"type":"img","src":"img/week06/solar_heater","alt":"Figure 6.16 — A thermosiphon solar water heater, common on Caribbean rooftops","caption":"Figure 6.16 — A thermosiphon solar water heater, common on Caribbean rooftops","width":5.4,"w":1174,"h":570},{"type":"bullets","items":["**Blackened copper pipes / black absorber plate:** dull black is the best absorber of the Sun's radiation; copper is a good conductor, so it passes heat quickly to the water inside.","**Glass cover:** lets the Sun's short-wavelength radiation in but traps the long-wavelength infrared emitted by the hot plate (a greenhouse effect); also stops convection currents carrying heat away.","**Insulation behind the panel and around the tank:** poor conductor (e.g. foam/fibreglass) reduces heat loss by conduction.","**Storage tank ABOVE the panel:** hot, less dense water rises by **convection** into the top of the tank; cold, denser water sinks from the bottom of the tank back to the panel. No pump is needed (thermosiphon).","Hot water is drawn off from the TOP of the tank, where the hottest water collects."]},{"type":"h","text":"3. The greenhouse and the greenhouse effect"},{"type":"img","src":"img/week06/greenhouse","alt":"Figure 6.17 — Radiation in a greenhouse","caption":"Figure 6.17 — Radiation in a greenhouse","width":4.6,"w":1054,"h":644},{"type":"steps","items":["The Sun is very hot, so it emits mainly **short-wavelength** radiation. This passes easily through the glass.","The soil and plants absorb it and warm up.","The warm soil and plants (much cooler than the Sun) emit **long-wavelength** infrared radiation.","Glass does NOT let long-wavelength infrared through — it reflects/absorbs it back inside. So energy is trapped and the greenhouse warms up.","Closed glass also stops warm air escaping by convection."]},{"type":"p","text":"**The greenhouse effect on Earth:** carbon dioxide, methane and water vapour in the atmosphere act like the glass, trapping the long-wavelength infrared emitted by the Earth. Burning fossil fuels increases CO~2~, trapping more energy → **global warming** → sea-level rise, stronger hurricanes and coral bleaching — serious issues for Caribbean islands. The same effect explains why a car parked in the sun with closed windows becomes very hot inside."},{"type":"h","text":"4. Cooking utensils"},{"type":"table","headers":["Part","Material","Reason"],"rows":[["Pot body","aluminium, copper or steel (metal)","good conductors: heat passes quickly from flame to food"],["Copper (or thick) base","copper","excellent conductor — spreads heat evenly, no hot spots"],["Handles","wood, plastic (Bakelite)","poor conductors — safe to hold"],["Outside of pot","dull/dark base absorbs; shiny sides","dark base absorbs radiation from the flame; shiny sides emit less, so less heat is lost"],["Lid","any","stops convection and evaporation losses → food cooks faster, saves fuel"]]},{"type":"h","text":"5. More applications"},{"type":"bullets","items":["**Light-coloured clothing** in hot weather reflects radiation; **layers of clothing** trap air (insulator).","**Ceiling insulation / double glazing** trap air or use a vacuum → reduce conduction and convection.","**Silver emergency (space) blankets** reflect body radiation back to a casualty.","**Cooler (ice) boxes:** expanded polystyrene contains trapped air (poor conductor); light-coloured outer surface reflects radiation.","**Fins on a motorcycle engine** increase surface area to lose heat faster."]},{"type":"tip","text":"A 4-mark \"explain the features of a vacuum flask\" question: give FOUR features, and for each name the method of heat transfer it reduces. E.g. \"vacuum — prevents conduction and convection because there are no particles\"."}],"examples":[{"id":"week06.6.6.e1","title":"Vacuum flask","question":"Explain how a vacuum flask keeps a cold drink cold. [4 marks]","steps":[{"label":"","text":"The vacuum between the walls has no particles, so heat from the surroundings cannot reach the drink by conduction or convection. ⟪(1+1)⟫"},{"label":"","text":"The silvered walls reflect radiation from the surroundings away from the drink (poor absorber). (1)"},{"label":"","text":"The stopper is a poor conductor and stops warm air entering by convection. (1)"}],"answer":"It reduces heat flowing IN by all three methods."},{"id":"week06.6.6.e2","title":"Solar water heater","question":"Explain why the storage tank of a solar water heater is placed higher than the collector panel.","steps":[{"label":"","text":"Water in the panel is heated, expands and becomes less dense."},{"label":"","text":"Less dense hot water rises up into the tank by convection, while colder denser water sinks back to the panel."},{"label":"","text":"This convection current circulates the water without a pump. If the tank were lower, the hot water could not rise into it."}],"answer":"Convection: hot water rises into the higher tank."}]},"tryit":[{"id":"week06.6.6.p1","kind":"self","q":"State the method of heat transfer reduced by (i) the vacuum, (ii) the silvered walls of a vacuum flask.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":2,"scheme":"(i) conduction and convection (1); (ii) radiation (1).","points":[{"text":"(i) conduction and convection","marks":1},{"text":"(ii) radiation","marks":1}],"section":"try","hint":{"text":"Look back at Example 1 (Vacuum flask). Start like this: The vacuum between the walls has no particles, so heat from the surroundings cannot reach the drink by conduction or convection. ⟪(1+1)⟫","from":1}},{"id":"week06.6.6.p2","kind":"self","q":"Why is the stopper of a vacuum flask made of cork or plastic?","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":2,"scheme":"Poor conductor, reduces conduction (1); seals the flask, preventing convection/evaporation from the top (1).","points":[{"text":"Poor conductor, reduces conduction","marks":1},{"text":"seals the flask, preventing convection/evaporation from the top","marks":1}],"section":"try","hint":{"text":"Look back at Example 1 (Vacuum flask). Start like this: The vacuum between the walls has no particles, so heat from the surroundings cannot reach the drink by conduction or convection. ⟪(1+1)⟫","from":1}}],"practice":[{"id":"week06.6.6.p3","kind":"self","q":"Explain why the absorber pipes of a solar water heater are painted dull black.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":2,"scheme":"Dull black is the best absorber of radiation (1), so more of the Sun's energy is absorbed and passed to the water (1).","points":[{"text":"Dull black is the best absorber of radiation","marks":1},{"text":"so more of the Sun's energy is absorbed and passed to the water","marks":1}],"section":"practice","hint":{"text":"Look back at Example 2 (Solar water heater). Start like this: Water in the panel is heated, expands and becomes less dense.","from":2}},{"id":"week06.6.6.p4","kind":"self","q":"Explain why the collector of a solar water heater has a glass cover.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":3,"scheme":"Glass lets the Sun's short-wavelength radiation through but traps the long-wavelength infrared emitted by the hot pipes (1); also reduces heat loss by convection (1).","points":[{"text":"Glass lets the Sun's short-wavelength radiation through but traps the long-wavelength infrared emitted by the hot pipes","marks":1},{"text":"also reduces heat loss by convection","marks":1}],"section":"practice","hint":{"text":"Look back at Example 2 (Solar water heater). Start like this: Water in the panel is heated, expands and becomes less dense.","from":2}},{"id":"week06.6.6.p5","kind":"self","q":"Explain how a greenhouse stays warmer than the air outside.","marks":4,"level":3,"lines":5,"scheme":"Short-wavelength radiation from the Sun passes through the glass (1); absorbed by soil/plants which warm up (1); they emit long-wavelength infrared (1); glass does not transmit it — reflected/absorbed back so energy is trapped (also no convection loss) (1).","points":[{"text":"Short-wavelength radiation from the Sun passes through the glass","marks":1},{"text":"absorbed by soil/plants which warm up","marks":1},{"text":"they emit long-wavelength infrared","marks":1},{"text":"glass does not transmit it — reflected/absorbed back so energy is trapped (also no convection loss)","marks":1}],"section":"practice","hint":{"text":"Look back at Example 2 (Solar water heater). Start like this: Water in the panel is heated, expands and becomes less dense.","from":2}},{"id":"week06.6.6.p6","kind":"self","q":"Explain why the inside of a car parked in the sun with closed windows becomes very hot.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":3,"scheme":"Short-wave radiation passes through windows, absorbed by seats which emit long-wave IR (1); IR cannot pass out through glass and hot air cannot escape by convection — energy trapped (greenhouse effect) (1).","points":[{"text":"Short-wave radiation passes through windows, absorbed by seats which emit long-wave IR","marks":1},{"text":"IR cannot pass out through glass and hot air cannot escape by convection — energy trapped (greenhouse effect)","marks":1}],"section":"practice"},{"id":"week06.6.6.p7","kind":"self","q":"A cooking pot has a copper base and a plastic handle. Give a reason for each.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":3,"scheme":"Copper: very good conductor — heat reaches food quickly/evenly (1). Plastic: poor conductor — handle stays cool/safe (1).","points":[{"text":"Copper: very good conductor — heat reaches food quickly/evenly","marks":1},{"text":"Plastic: poor conductor — handle stays cool/safe","marks":1}],"section":"practice"}],"exit":[{"id":"week06.6.6.p8","kind":"self","q":"Give ONE reason why people in hot countries often wear white or light-coloured clothes.","marks":1,"level":1,"lines":2,"scheme":"Light colours are poor absorbers/good reflectors of radiation, so the body absorbs less heat (1).","points":[{"text":"Light colours are poor absorbers/good reflectors of radiation, so the body absorbs less heat","marks":1}],"section":"exit"},{"id":"week06.6.6.p9","kind":"self","q":"Explain why putting a lid on a pot saves fuel when cooking.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":3,"scheme":"Lid stops hot air/steam escaping (reduces convection and evaporation losses) (1), so less energy is wasted and food heats faster (1).","points":[{"text":"Lid stops hot air/steam escaping (reduces convection and evaporation losses)","marks":1},{"text":"so less energy is wasted and food heats faster","marks":1}],"section":"exit"}],"warmup":[{"id":"week06.6.6.w1","kind":"self","q":"In the absorption experiment (Figure 6.13), why must the heater be placed exactly midway between the plates?","marks":1,"level":1,"lines":2,"scheme":"So both plates receive the same amount of radiation (fair test) (1).","points":[{"text":"So both plates receive the same amount of radiation (fair test)","marks":1}],"section":"warmup","src":"week06.6.5.p5"},{"id":"week06.6.6.w2","kind":"self","q":"State the relationship between how well a surface absorbs radiation and how well it emits radiation.","marks":1,"level":1,"lines":2,"scheme":"Good absorbers are good emitters (poor absorbers are poor emitters) (1).","points":[{"text":"Good absorbers are good emitters (poor absorbers are poor emitters)","marks":1}],"section":"warmup","src":"week06.6.5.p6"}],"n":43},{"id":"week06.6.7","kind":"unit","unit":"6.7","title":"Checkpoint Test: Sections A & B (40 marks)","titleText":"Checkpoint Test: Sections A & B (40 marks)","goals":[],"codes":["Review of A1–A6","B1–B4"],"words":[],"learn":{"notes":[{"type":"p","text":"This test checks everything from Weeks 1–6: measurement, forces, motion, energy, pressure and thermal physics. It is written in CSEC style: **15 multiple-choice questions (15 marks)** followed by **4 structured questions (25 marks)**."},{"type":"table","headers":["Instructions",""],"rows":[["Time allowed","60 minutes. Write your start time: ______  finish time: ______"],["Conditions","No notes, no workbook pages, no phone. A calculator and ruler are allowed."],["Constants","⟪g=10\\,\\mathrm{N}\\,\\mathrm{kg}^{-1}⟫; c(water) ⟪=4200\\,\\mathrm{J}\\,\\mathrm{kg}^{-1}\\,{}^{\\circ}\\mathrm{C}^{-1}⟫"],["MCQ (1–15)","Circle ONE letter. About 1 minute each."],["Structured (16–19)","Write on the lines. Show ALL working: Formula → Substitute → Answer with unit."],["After the test","Do NOT mark it yourself. Bring it to the next session; your tutor will mark it and fill in the table below."]]},{"type":"warn","text":"Do this test only AFTER finishing Units 6.1–6.6. Revise your formula cards from Weeks 1–5 the day before. Do not look at the notes during the test — the result must show what you really know.","label":"TEST CONDITIONS"},{"type":"h","text":"Score record (tutor fills in)"},{"type":"table","headers":["Part","Topic","Marks available","Score"],"rows":[["MCQ 1–9","Section A: measurement, forces, motion, energy, pressure","9",""],["MCQ 10–15","Section A (Newton) & Section B: heat, temperature, latent heat","6",""],["Q16","Velocity–time graph, ⟪F=ma⟫","8",""],["Q17","Moments","6",""],["Q18","Energy conservation","6",""],["Q19","Latent heat and power","5",""],["TOTAL","","40",""]]},{"type":"p","text":"**Target:** 24/40 (60%) or more. Any section below half marks goes on your Mistake Log and into the weak-topic drill in Week 13."}],"examples":[]},"tryit":[{"id":"week06.6.7.p1","kind":"mcq","q":"**[MCQ 1]** Which of the following is a VECTOR quantity?","marks":1,"level":1,"options":["mass","speed","acceleration","energy"],"answer":"C","explanation":"Acceleration has size and direction (1).","section":"try"},{"id":"week06.6.7.p2","kind":"mcq","q":"**[MCQ 2]** A stone has mass 200 g and volume 80 cm^3^. Its density is","marks":1,"level":1,"options":["0.40 g cm^−3^","2.5 g cm^−3^","120 g cm^−3^","16 000 g cm^−3^"],"answer":"B","explanation":"⟪\\rho =m/V=200\\div 80=2.5\\,\\mathrm{g}\\,\\mathrm{cm}^{-3}⟫ (1).","section":"try"}],"practice":[{"id":"week06.6.7.p3","kind":"mcq","q":"**[MCQ 3]** The weight of a 5.0 kg bag on Earth ⟪(g=10\\,\\mathrm{N}\\,\\mathrm{kg}^{-1})⟫ is","marks":1,"level":1,"options":["0.5 N","5.0 N","15 N","50 N"],"answer":"D","explanation":"⟪W=mg=5.0\\times 10=50\\,\\mathrm{N}⟫ (1).","section":"practice"},{"id":"week06.6.7.p4","kind":"mcq","q":"**[MCQ 4]** A spring has spring constant 40 N m^−1^. The extension produced by a 6.0 N load is","marks":1,"level":1,"options":["0.15 m","6.7 m","34 m","240 m"],"answer":"A","explanation":"⟪x=F/k=6.0\\div 40=0.15\\,\\mathrm{m}⟫ (1).","section":"practice"},{"id":"week06.6.7.p5","kind":"mcq","q":"**[MCQ 5]** A 2.0 kg trolley moving at 3.0 m s^−1^ hits and sticks to a stationary 1.0 kg trolley. Their common velocity is","marks":1,"level":1,"options":["1.0 m s^−1^","1.5 m s^−1^","2.0 m s^−1^","3.0 m s^−1^"],"answer":"C","explanation":"Momentum before ⟪=2.0\\times 3.0=6.0\\,\\mathrm{kg}\\,\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-1}⟫; ⟪v=6.0\\div 3.0=2.0\\,\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-1}⟫ (1).","section":"practice"},{"id":"week06.6.7.p6","kind":"mcq","q":"**[MCQ 6]** The kinetic energy of a 2.0 kg ball moving at 5.0 m s^−1^ is","marks":1,"level":1,"options":["5.0 J","10 J","25 J","50 J"],"answer":"C","explanation":"⟪Ek=\\tfrac{1}{2}\\times 2.0\\times 5.0^{2}=25\\,\\mathrm{J}⟫ (1).","section":"practice"},{"id":"week06.6.7.p7","kind":"mcq","q":"**[MCQ 7]** A motor does 600 J of work in 20 s. Its power is","marks":1,"level":1,"options":["0.033 W","30 W","580 W","12 000 W"],"answer":"B","explanation":"⟪P=E/t=600\\div 20=30\\,\\mathrm{W}⟫ (1).","section":"practice"},{"id":"week06.6.7.p8","kind":"mcq","q":"**[MCQ 8]** The pressure due to water at a depth of 5.0 m (ρ = 1000 kg m^−3^, ⟪g=10\\,\\mathrm{N}\\,\\mathrm{kg}^{-1}⟫) is","marks":1,"level":1,"options":["50 Pa","200 Pa","5000 Pa","50 000 Pa"],"answer":"D","explanation":"⟪p=\\rho gh=1000\\times 10\\times 5.0=50\\,000\\,\\mathrm{Pa}⟫ (1).","section":"practice"},{"id":"week06.6.7.p9","kind":"mcq","q":"**[MCQ 9]** A machine gives 80 J of useful energy for every 200 J supplied. Its efficiency is","marks":1,"level":1,"options":["2.5%","40%","60%","120%"],"answer":"B","explanation":"⟪80\\div 200\\times 100=40\\%⟫ (1).","section":"practice"},{"id":"week06.6.7.p10","kind":"mcq","q":"**[MCQ 10]** A book rests on a table. According to Newton's third law, the reaction to the weight of the book is","marks":1,"level":1,"options":["the push of the table on the book","the pull of the book on the Earth","the friction on the book","the push of the book on the table"],"answer":"B","explanation":"Weight = Earth pulls book; the pair is book pulls Earth (same type of force, acting on the other body) (1).","section":"practice"},{"id":"week06.6.7.p11","kind":"mcq","q":"**[MCQ 11]** Joule's experiments showed that","marks":1,"level":1,"options":["heat is a fluid called caloric","a fixed amount of work always produces the same amount of heat","heat cannot travel through a vacuum","temperature depends on mass"],"answer":"B","explanation":"Joule showed mechanical work converts to heat in a fixed ratio — heat is a form of energy (1).","section":"practice"},{"id":"week06.6.7.p12","kind":"mcq","q":"**[MCQ 12]** The constriction in a clinical thermometer","marks":1,"level":1,"options":["makes it more sensitive","stops the mercury falling back before the reading is taken","increases its range","lets it respond faster"],"answer":"B","explanation":"The narrow kink breaks the thread so the maximum reading stays (1).","section":"practice"},{"id":"week06.6.7.p13","kind":"mcq","q":"**[MCQ 13]** The energy needed to raise the temperature of 2.0 kg of water by ⟪10\\,{}^{\\circ}\\mathrm{C}\\ (c=4200\\,\\mathrm{J}\\,\\mathrm{kg}^{-1}\\,{}^{\\circ}\\mathrm{C}^{-1})⟫ is","marks":1,"level":1,"options":["8 400 J","21 000 J","42 000 J","84 000 J"],"answer":"D","explanation":"⟪E=mc\\Delta \\theta =2.0\\times 4200\\times 10=84\\,000\\,\\mathrm{J}⟫ (1).","section":"practice"},{"id":"week06.6.7.p14","kind":"mcq","q":"**[MCQ 14]** While a solid is melting, its temperature stays constant because the energy supplied","marks":1,"level":1,"options":["is lost to the surroundings","increases the kinetic energy of the particles","is used to break the bonds between particles","is stored as heat capacity"],"answer":"C","explanation":"Latent heat breaks bonds (increases PE), not KE, so temperature is constant (1).","section":"practice"},{"id":"week06.6.7.p15","kind":"mcq","q":"**[MCQ 15]** Which statement about evaporation is correct?","marks":1,"level":1,"options":["It occurs only at the boiling point","It occurs throughout the liquid","It occurs at any temperature, from the surface only","It produces bubbles in the liquid"],"answer":"C","explanation":"Evaporation: any temperature, surface only; boiling: fixed temperature, throughout (1).","section":"practice"},{"id":"week06.6.7.p16","kind":"self","q":"**Question 16** (8 marks). The velocity–time graph shows the motion of a car of mass 800 kg.  **(a)** Describe the motion of the car in sections A, B and C.","marks":2,"level":2,"img":{"src":"img/week06/ckpt_vt","alt":"Question 16 (8 marks). The velocity–time graph shows the motion of a car of mass 800 kg.  (a) Describe the motion of the car in sections A, B and C.","w":974,"h":574},"lines":3,"scheme":"A: uniform (constant) acceleration from rest; B: constant velocity of 12 m s⁻¹; C: uniform deceleration to rest. (All three correct ⟪=2⟫; two correct ⟪=1⟫.)","points":[{"text":"A: uniform (constant) acceleration from rest; B: constant velocity of 12 m s⁻¹; C: uniform deceleration to rest. (All three correct ⟪=2⟫; two correct ⟪=1⟫.)","marks":2}],"conf":"low","section":"practice"},{"id":"week06.6.7.p17","kind":"num","q":"**Q16 (b)** Calculate the acceleration of the car in section A.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":3,"scheme":"⟪a=\\Delta v/\\Delta t=12\\div 4⟫ (1) ⟪=3.0\\,\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-2}⟫ (1).","points":[{"text":"⟪a=\\Delta v/\\Delta t=12\\div 4⟫","marks":1},{"text":"⟪=3.0\\,\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-2}⟫","marks":1}],"num":{"value":3,"sf":2,"unit":"m s^−2^","unitTex":"\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-2}"},"section":"practice"},{"id":"week06.6.7.p18","kind":"num","q":"**Q16 (c)** Calculate the total distance travelled in the 14 s.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":3,"scheme":"Distance = area under graph ⟪=\\tfrac{1}{2}\\times 4\\times 12+6\\times 12+\\tfrac{1}{2}\\times 4\\times 12⟫ (1) ⟪=24+72+24=120\\,\\mathrm{m}⟫ (1).","points":[{"text":"Distance = area under graph ⟪=\\tfrac{1}{2}\\times 4\\times 12+6\\times 12+\\tfrac{1}{2}\\times 4\\times 12⟫","marks":1},{"text":"⟪=24+72+24=120\\,\\mathrm{m}⟫","marks":1}],"num":{"value":120,"sf":2,"unit":"m","unitTex":"\\mathrm{m}"},"section":"practice"},{"id":"week06.6.7.p19","kind":"num","q":"**Q16 (d)** Calculate the resultant force on the car in section A.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":2,"scheme":"⟪F=ma=800\\times 3.0⟫ (1) ⟪=2400\\,\\mathrm{N}⟫ (1).","points":[{"text":"⟪F=ma=800\\times 3.0⟫","marks":1},{"text":"⟪=2400\\,\\mathrm{N}⟫","marks":1}],"num":{"value":2400,"sf":2,"unit":"N","unitTex":"\\mathrm{N}"},"section":"practice"},{"id":"week06.6.7.p20","kind":"self","q":"**Question 17** (6 marks). A uniform metre rule is balanced at its 50 cm mark. A 3.0 N weight hangs at the 10 cm mark and an unknown weight W at the 80 cm mark.  **(a)** State the principle of moments.","marks":2,"level":2,"img":{"src":"img/week06/ckpt_beam","alt":"Question 17 (6 marks). A uniform metre rule is balanced at its 50 cm mark. A 3.0 N weight hangs at the 10 cm mark and an unknown weight W at the 80 cm mark.  (a) State the principle of moments.","w":1094,"h":214},"lines":3,"scheme":"For a body in equilibrium (1), the sum of clockwise moments about any point = the sum of anticlockwise moments about that point (1).","points":[{"text":"For a body in equilibrium","marks":1},{"text":"the sum of clockwise moments about any point = the sum of anticlockwise moments about that point","marks":1}],"section":"practice"},{"id":"week06.6.7.p21","kind":"num","q":"**Q17 (b)** Calculate W.","marks":3,"level":2,"lines":4,"scheme":"Anticlockwise moment ⟪=3.0\\times 40=120\\,\\mathrm{N}\\,\\mathrm{cm}⟫ (1); clockwise ⟪=W\\times 30⟫ (1); ⟪W=120\\div 30=4.0\\,\\mathrm{N}⟫ (1).","points":[{"text":"Anticlockwise moment ⟪=3.0\\times 40=120\\,\\mathrm{N}\\,\\mathrm{cm}⟫","marks":1},{"text":"clockwise ⟪=W\\times 30⟫","marks":1},{"text":"⟪W=120\\div 30=4.0\\,\\mathrm{N}⟫","marks":1}],"num":{"value":4,"sf":2,"unit":"N","unitTex":"\\mathrm{N}"},"section":"practice"},{"id":"week06.6.7.p22","kind":"self","q":"**Q17 (c)** State the upward force exerted by the pivot (ignore the weight of the rule).","marks":1,"level":1,"lines":1,"scheme":"⟪3.0+4.0=7.0\\,\\mathrm{N}⟫ (1).","points":[{"text":"⟪3.0+4.0=7.0\\,\\mathrm{N}⟫","marks":1}],"section":"practice"},{"id":"week06.6.7.p23","kind":"self","q":"**Question 18** (6 marks). A ball of mass 0.50 kg is dropped from a height of 20 m. Ignore air resistance.  **(a)** State the law of conservation of energy.","marks":1,"level":1,"lines":2,"scheme":"Energy cannot be created or destroyed, only changed from one form to another (total energy is constant) (1).","points":[{"text":"Energy cannot be created or destroyed, only changed from one form to another (total energy is constant)","marks":1}],"section":"practice"},{"id":"week06.6.7.p24","kind":"num","q":"**Q18 (b)** Calculate the gravitational potential energy lost by the ball as it falls to the ground.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":2,"scheme":"⟪\\Delta Ep=mgh=0.50\\times 10\\times 20⟫ (1) ⟪=100\\,\\mathrm{J}⟫ (1).","points":[{"text":"⟪\\Delta Ep=mgh=0.50\\times 10\\times 20⟫","marks":1},{"text":"⟪=100\\,\\mathrm{J}⟫","marks":1}],"num":{"value":100,"sf":1,"unit":"J","unitTex":"\\mathrm{J}"},"section":"practice"},{"id":"week06.6.7.p25","kind":"num","q":"**Q18 (c)** Calculate the speed of the ball just before it hits the ground.","marks":3,"level":2,"lines":4,"scheme":"KE gained ⟪=PE⟫ lost ⟪=100\\,\\mathrm{J}⟫ (1); ⟪\\tfrac{1}{2}\\times 0.50\\times v^{2}=100\\rightarrow v^{2}=400⟫ (1); ⟪v=20\\,\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-1}⟫ (1).","points":[{"text":"KE gained ⟪=PE⟫ lost ⟪=100\\,\\mathrm{J}⟫","marks":1},{"text":"⟪\\tfrac{1}{2}\\times 0.50\\times v^{2}=100\\rightarrow v^{2}=400⟫","marks":1},{"text":"⟪v=20\\,\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-1}⟫","marks":1}],"num":{"value":20,"sf":1,"unit":"m s^−1^","unitTex":"\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-1}"},"conf":"low","section":"practice"},{"id":"week06.6.7.p26","kind":"self","q":"**Question 19** (5 marks). 0.20 kg of ice at 0 °C is melted by a 50 W heater. Specific latent heat of fusion of ice ⟪=3.4\\times 10^{5}\\,\\mathrm{J}\\,\\mathrm{kg}^{-1}⟫.  **(a)** Define specific latent heat of fusion.","marks":1,"level":1,"lines":2,"scheme":"Energy needed to change 1 kg of a solid to liquid without a change in temperature (1).","points":[{"text":"Energy needed to change 1 kg of a solid to liquid without a change in temperature","marks":1}],"section":"practice"}],"exit":[{"id":"week06.6.7.p27","kind":"num","q":"**Q19 (b)** Calculate the energy needed to melt the ice.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":2,"scheme":"⟪E=ml=0.20\\times 3.4\\times 10^{5}⟫ (1) ⟪=6.8\\times 10^{4}\\,\\mathrm{J}\\ (68\\,000\\,\\mathrm{J})⟫ (1).","points":[{"text":"⟪E=ml=0.20\\times 3.4\\times 10^{5}⟫","marks":1},{"text":"⟪=6.8\\times 10^{4}\\,\\mathrm{J}\\ (68\\,000\\,\\mathrm{J})⟫","marks":1}],"num":{"value":68000,"sf":2,"unit":"J","unitTex":"\\mathrm{J}"},"section":"exit"},{"id":"week06.6.7.p28","kind":"self","q":"**Q19 (c)** Calculate the time the heater takes to supply this energy (assume no heat is lost).","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":2,"scheme":"⟪t=E/P=68\\,000\\div 50⟫ (1) ⟪=1360\\,\\mathrm{s}\\ (\\approx 22.7\\,\\mathrm{min})⟫ (1).","points":[{"text":"⟪t=E/P=68\\,000\\div 50⟫","marks":1},{"text":"⟪=1360\\,\\mathrm{s}\\ (\\approx 22.7\\,\\mathrm{min})⟫","marks":1}],"section":"exit"}],"warmup":[{"id":"week06.6.7.w1","kind":"self","q":"State the method of heat transfer reduced by (i) the vacuum, (ii) the silvered walls of a vacuum flask.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":2,"scheme":"(i) conduction and convection (1); (ii) radiation (1).","points":[{"text":"(i) conduction and convection","marks":1},{"text":"(ii) radiation","marks":1}],"section":"warmup","src":"week06.6.6.p1"},{"id":"week06.6.7.w2","kind":"self","q":"Why is the stopper of a vacuum flask made of cork or plastic?","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":2,"scheme":"Poor conductor, reduces conduction (1); seals the flask, preventing convection/evaporation from the top (1).","points":[{"text":"Poor conductor, reduces conduction","marks":1},{"text":"seals the flask, preventing convection/evaporation from the top","marks":1}],"section":"warmup","src":"week06.6.6.p2"},{"id":"week06.6.7.w3","kind":"self","q":"Give ONE reason why people in hot countries often wear white or light-coloured clothes.","marks":1,"level":1,"lines":2,"scheme":"Light colours are poor absorbers/good reflectors of radiation, so the body absorbs less heat (1).","points":[{"text":"Light colours are poor absorbers/good reflectors of radiation, so the body absorbs less heat","marks":1}],"section":"warmup","src":"week06.6.6.p8"}],"n":44},{"id":"week06.check","kind":"check","title":"Book check","titleText":"Book check","check":true,"watchOut":["Using °C instead of kelvin in gas-law calculations. ALWAYS add 273 first.","Adding 273 to a temperature CHANGE. A rise of 30 °C is a rise of 30 K.","Writing °K. The unit is simply K (kelvin).","Mixing up the laws: Boyle is INVERSE (⟪p\\times V⟫ constant); Charles and the pressure law are DIRECT (divide by T).","Saying gas particles get bigger when heated. The SPACES between them increase; the particles move faster.","Forgetting \"per unit area\" and \"per second\" in kinetic-theory explanations of pressure.","Writing \"heat rises\". Write \"hot air/water rises because it is less dense\".","Saying metal objects are colder than wooden ones at the same temperature. They conduct heat away from your hand faster.","Saying the vacuum in a flask stops radiation. The vacuum stops conduction and convection; the SILVERED walls reduce radiation.","Confusing absorbers and reflectors: shiny surfaces REFLECT radiation; dull black surfaces ABSORB (and emit) it."],"mcq":[{"id":"week06.check.m1","kind":"mcq","q":"Absolute zero on the Celsius scale is","marks":1,"level":1,"options":["−273 °C","−100 °C","0 °C","273 °C"],"answer":"A","explanation":"⟪0\\,\\mathrm{K}=-273\\,{}^{\\circ}\\mathrm{C}⟫. C (0 °C) is the ice point ⟪=273\\,\\mathrm{K}⟫; D is the ice point in kelvin with the wrong unit.","section":"p01"},{"id":"week06.check.m2","kind":"mcq","q":"A temperature of 47 °C is equal to","marks":1,"level":1,"options":["226 K","273 K","47 K","320 K"],"answer":"D","explanation":"⟪T=47+273=320\\,\\mathrm{K}⟫. A comes from subtracting ⟪(273-47)⟫.","section":"p01"},{"id":"week06.check.m3","kind":"mcq","q":"A fixed mass of gas at 100 kPa has volume 120 cm^3^. At constant temperature it is compressed to 60 cm^3^. The new pressure is","marks":1,"level":1,"options":["50 kPa","100 kPa","200 kPa","7200 kPa"],"answer":"C","explanation":"⟪p_{2}=100\\times 120\\div 60=200\\,\\mathrm{kPa}⟫ (halve V → double p). A uses direct, not inverse, proportion.","section":"p01"},{"id":"week06.check.m4","kind":"mcq","q":"For a fixed mass of gas at constant temperature, which graph is a straight line through the origin?","marks":1,"level":1,"options":["p against V","p against 1/V","V against T","pV against p"],"answer":"B","explanation":"Boyle: ⟪p\\propto 1/V⟫. p against V is a curve; pV against p is a horizontal line (not through origin).","section":"p01"},{"id":"week06.check.m5","kind":"mcq","q":"At constant pressure, a gas at 27 °C will double its volume when heated to","marks":1,"level":1,"options":["54 °C","327 °C","300 °C","600 °C"],"answer":"B","explanation":"⟪300\\,\\mathrm{K}\\times 2=600\\,\\mathrm{K}=327\\,{}^{\\circ}\\mathrm{C}⟫. A doubles the Celsius value — the classic error; D is 600 in kelvin labelled °C.","section":"p01"},{"id":"week06.check.m6","kind":"mcq","q":"When a gas in a sealed rigid container is heated, its pressure rises because the particles","marks":1,"level":1,"options":["become larger","move faster and hit the walls more often and harder","attract each other more strongly","move closer together"],"answer":"B","explanation":"Faster particles → more frequent, harder collisions → greater force per unit area. Particles do not grow (A); V is fixed so spacing is unchanged (D).","section":"p01"},{"id":"week06.check.m7","kind":"mcq","q":"The temperature of a liquid rises by 30 °C. The rise in kelvin is","marks":1,"level":1,"options":["30 K","243 K","303 K","⟪-243\\,\\mathrm{K}⟫"],"answer":"A","explanation":"A temperature CHANGE is the same on both scales. C adds 273 to a change — wrong.","section":"p01"},{"id":"week06.check.m8","kind":"mcq","q":"Which material is the BEST conductor of heat?","marks":1,"level":1,"options":["copper","air","glass","wood"],"answer":"A","explanation":"Metals have free electrons; copper is an excellent conductor. Air (B) is one of the poorest.","section":"p01"},{"id":"week06.check.m9","kind":"mcq","q":"Convection can take place in","marks":1,"level":1,"options":["solids only","liquids and gases only","a vacuum","solids and liquids only"],"answer":"B","explanation":"Convection needs a fluid that can flow. Solids cannot flow; a vacuum has no particles.","section":"p01"},{"id":"week06.check.m10","kind":"mcq","q":"Energy from the Sun reaches the Earth by","marks":1,"level":1,"options":["conduction","convection","evaporation","radiation"],"answer":"D","explanation":"Space is a vacuum: only electromagnetic radiation can cross it.","section":"p01"},{"id":"week06.check.m11","kind":"mcq","q":"Which surface is the best EMITTER of infrared radiation?","marks":1,"level":1,"options":["dull black","white gloss","shiny silver","polished aluminium"],"answer":"A","explanation":"Dull black = best absorber and best emitter. Shiny surfaces are the worst emitters (best reflectors).","section":"p01"},{"id":"week06.check.m12","kind":"mcq","q":"The silvered surfaces of a vacuum flask reduce heat transfer by","marks":1,"level":1,"options":["conduction","convection","radiation","conduction and convection"],"answer":"C","explanation":"Shiny surfaces reflect radiation and are poor emitters. The VACUUM reduces conduction and convection (D).","section":"p01"},{"id":"week06.check.m13","kind":"mcq","q":"During a sunny day a sea breeze blows from the","marks":1,"level":1,"options":["land to the sea because the sea is warmer","sea to the land because the sea is warmer","land to the sea because the land is warmer","sea to the land because the land is warmer"],"answer":"D","explanation":"Land heats faster; warm air over land rises; cooler air from the sea moves in. A/C describe the night land breeze direction.","section":"p01"},{"id":"week06.check.m14","kind":"mcq","q":"A greenhouse stays warm mainly because glass","marks":1,"level":1,"options":["absorbs all radiation from the Sun","lets short-wavelength radiation in but not long-wavelength infrared out","is a good conductor of heat","emits more radiation than it absorbs"],"answer":"B","explanation":"Short-wave in, long-wave IR from warm soil trapped. Glass is a poor conductor (C wrong).","section":"p01"},{"id":"week06.check.m15","kind":"mcq","q":"A fixed mass of gas has volume 3.0 L. Its pressure is doubled AND its kelvin temperature is doubled. The new volume is","marks":1,"level":1,"options":["0.75 L","1.5 L","3.0 L","6.0 L"],"answer":"C","explanation":"⟪V_{2}=V_{1}\\times (p_{1}/p_{2})\\times (T_{2}/T_{1})=3.0\\times \\tfrac{1}{2}\\times 2=3.0\\,\\mathrm{L}⟫. B considers pressure only; D considers temperature only.","section":"p01"}],"structured":[{"id":"week06.check.s1","kind":"multi","title":"Gas in a sealed canister","total":12,"stem":["A rigid metal canister contains a fixed mass of gas at a pressure of 150 kPa when its temperature is 27 °C."],"stemBlocks":[],"parts":[{"id":"week06.check.s1.a","kind":"self","q":"State the pressure law.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":3,"scheme":"Pressure of a fixed mass of gas is directly proportional to its absolute (kelvin) temperature (1), provided the volume is constant (1).","points":[{"text":"Pressure of a fixed mass of gas is directly proportional to its absolute (kelvin) temperature","marks":1},{"text":"provided the volume is constant","marks":1}],"label":"(a)"},{"id":"week06.check.s1.b","kind":"self","q":"Convert 27 °C to kelvin.","marks":1,"level":1,"lines":1,"scheme":"⟪27+273=300\\,\\mathrm{K}⟫ (1).","points":[{"text":"⟪27+273=300\\,\\mathrm{K}⟫","marks":1}],"num":{"value":300,"sf":1,"unit":"K","unitTex":"\\mathrm{K}"},"label":"(b)","autoKind":"num"},{"id":"week06.check.s1.c","kind":"self","q":"The canister is left in the sun and its temperature rises to 87 °C. Calculate the new pressure of the gas.","marks":3,"level":2,"lines":4,"scheme":"⟪T_{2}=360\\,\\mathrm{K}⟫ (1); ⟪p_{2}=p_{1}T_{2}/T_{1}=150\\times 360\\div 300⟫ (1) ⟪=180\\,\\mathrm{kPa}⟫ (1).","points":[{"text":"⟪T_{2}=360\\,\\mathrm{K}⟫","marks":1},{"text":"⟪p_{2}=p_{1}T_{2}/T_{1}=150\\times 360\\div 300⟫","marks":1},{"text":"⟪=180\\,\\mathrm{kPa}⟫","marks":1}],"num":{"value":180,"sf":2,"unit":"kPa","unitTex":"\\mathrm{kPa}"},"label":"(c)","autoKind":"num"},{"id":"week06.check.s1.d","kind":"self","q":"Use the kinetic theory to explain why the pressure increases.","marks":3,"level":2,"lines":4,"scheme":"Particles gain KE / move faster (1); collide with walls more often and with greater force (1); volume constant so greater force per unit area = greater pressure (1).","points":[{"text":"Particles gain KE / move faster","marks":1},{"text":"collide with walls more often and with greater force","marks":1},{"text":"volume constant so greater force per unit area = greater pressure","marks":1}],"label":"(d)"},{"id":"week06.check.s1.e","kind":"self","q":"Sketch a graph of pressure against temperature in °C for this gas, and mark the temperature at which the pressure would become zero.","marks":3,"level":2,"lines":6,"scheme":"Axes labelled p and θ/°C (1); straight line with positive gradient (1); extrapolated (dashed) to cut θ-axis at −273 °C, marked (1).","points":[{"text":"Axes labelled p and θ/°C","marks":1},{"text":"straight line with positive gradient","marks":1},{"text":"extrapolated (dashed) to cut θ-axis at −273 °C, marked","marks":1}],"label":"(e)"}]},{"id":"week06.check.s2","kind":"multi","title":"Cooling cans","total":12,"stem":["A student fills two identical metal cans, X and Y, with equal volumes of hot water at 80 °C. One can is painted dull black and the other is polished shiny. Lids are placed on both cans and the temperatures are recorded every minute. The graph shows the results."],"stemBlocks":[{"type":"img","src":"img/week06/cooling_cans","alt":"Cooling curves for cans X and Y","caption":"Cooling curves for cans X and Y","width":4.2,"w":1014,"h":634}],"parts":[{"id":"week06.check.s2.a","kind":"self","q":"Name the method of heat transfer that this experiment is designed to compare.","marks":1,"level":1,"lines":1,"scheme":"Radiation (1).","points":[{"text":"Radiation","marks":1}],"label":"(a)"},{"id":"week06.check.s2.b","kind":"self","q":"State which can is painted dull black. Give a reason for your answer.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":3,"scheme":"X (1); it cools faster — dull black is the better emitter of radiation (1).","points":[{"text":"X","marks":1},{"text":"it cools faster — dull black is the better emitter of radiation","marks":1}],"label":"(b)"},{"id":"week06.check.s2.c","kind":"self","q":"Use the graph to find the temperature of each can after 20 minutes, and the difference between them.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":3,"scheme":"⟪X\\approx 31\\,{}^{\\circ}\\mathrm{C}⟫ and ⟪Y\\approx 45\\,{}^{\\circ}\\mathrm{C}⟫ (accept ±2) (1); difference ≈ 14 °C (consistent with readings) (1).","points":[{"text":"⟪X\\approx 31\\,{}^{\\circ}\\mathrm{C}⟫ and ⟪Y\\approx 45\\,{}^{\\circ}\\mathrm{C}⟫ (accept ±2)","marks":1},{"text":"difference ≈ 14 °C (consistent with readings)","marks":1}],"label":"(c)"},{"id":"week06.check.s2.d","kind":"self","q":"State TWO variables, other than the volume of water, that were kept constant.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":2,"scheme":"Any two: same starting temperature; same size/material of can; lids on both; same surroundings/room temperature (1 each).","points":[{"text":"Any two: same starting temperature; same size/material of can; lids on both; same surroundings/room temperature (1 each).","marks":2,"each":true}],"label":"(d)"},{"id":"week06.check.s2.e","kind":"self","q":"Why were lids placed on the cans?","marks":1,"level":1,"lines":2,"scheme":"To reduce heat loss by convection/evaporation from the water surface, so only radiation differences are compared (1).","points":[{"text":"To reduce heat loss by convection/evaporation from the water surface, so only radiation differences are compared","marks":1}],"label":"(e)"},{"id":"week06.check.s2.f","kind":"self","q":"The two cans are now filled with cold water and placed in sunlight. Predict which can will warm up faster, and state the principle you used.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":3,"scheme":"X / dull black can (1); good emitters are good absorbers (1).","points":[{"text":"X / dull black can","marks":1},{"text":"good emitters are good absorbers","marks":1}],"label":"(f)"},{"id":"week06.check.s2.g","kind":"self","q":"Suggest why both cans cool more slowly as time goes on.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":3,"scheme":"The temperature difference between the can and the surroundings becomes smaller (1), so the rate of heat loss decreases (1).","points":[{"text":"The temperature difference between the can and the surroundings becomes smaller","marks":1},{"text":"so the rate of heat loss decreases","marks":1}],"label":"(g)"}]},{"id":"week06.check.s3","kind":"multi","title":"Keeping hot and cold","total":11,"stem":["Vacuum flasks and solar water heaters both make use of the three methods of heat transfer."],"stemBlocks":[],"parts":[{"id":"week06.check.s3.a","kind":"self","q":"Name FOUR features of a vacuum flask and state how each one reduces heat transfer.","marks":4,"level":3,"lines":6,"scheme":"Any four, each with correct method: vacuum — prevents conduction and convection (1); silvered walls — reduce radiation (1); stopper — poor conductor, stops convection/evaporation (1); insulating supports — reduce conduction (1); (outer case — protection: only credit if linked to insulation).","points":[{"text":"Any four, each with correct method: vacuum — prevents conduction and convection","marks":1},{"text":"silvered walls — reduce radiation","marks":1},{"text":"stopper — poor conductor, stops convection/evaporation","marks":1},{"text":"insulating supports — reduce conduction (outer case — protection: only credit if linked to insulation)","marks":1}],"label":"(a)"},{"id":"week06.check.s3.b","kind":"self","q":"In a solar water heater, explain why the pipes in the collector are made of copper and painted dull black.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":3,"scheme":"Dull black is the best absorber of radiation (1); copper is a good conductor, so heat passes quickly to the water (1).","points":[{"text":"Dull black is the best absorber of radiation","marks":1},{"text":"copper is a good conductor, so heat passes quickly to the water","marks":1}],"label":"(b)"},{"id":"week06.check.s3.c","kind":"self","q":"Explain why the storage tank is placed above the collector panel.","marks":3,"level":2,"lines":4,"scheme":"Water heated in the panel expands, becomes less dense (1); rises by convection into the tank (1); cooler denser water sinks back to the panel — circulation without a pump (1).","points":[{"text":"Water heated in the panel expands, becomes less dense","marks":1},{"text":"rises by convection into the tank","marks":1},{"text":"cooler denser water sinks back to the panel — circulation without a pump","marks":1}],"label":"(c)"},{"id":"week06.check.s3.d","kind":"self","q":"Explain why the collector has a glass cover.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":3,"scheme":"Short-wave solar radiation passes through, but long-wave IR emitted by the hot pipes is trapped (greenhouse effect) (1); also stops convection heat loss to the air (1).","points":[{"text":"Short-wave solar radiation passes through, but long-wave IR emitted by the hot pipes is trapped (greenhouse effect)","marks":1},{"text":"also stops convection heat loss to the air","marks":1}],"label":"(d)"}]}],"n":45},{"id":"week06.past","kind":"past","title":"Past-paper practice","titleText":"Past-paper practice","check":true,"log":true,"questions":[{"id":"week06.past.q1","kind":"multi","title":"Data analysis — the pressure law (Paper 02 Question 1 style)","total":22,"stem":["A student investigated how the pressure, p, of a fixed mass of dry air at constant volume varies with its temperature, θ. The flask of air was placed in a water bath and connected to a pressure gauge. The results are shown below."],"stemBlocks":[{"type":"table","headers":["Temperature, θ / °C","0","20","40","60","80","100"],"rows":[["Pressure, p / kPa","98","105","113","120","127","134"]]}],"parts":[{"id":"week06.past.q1.a","kind":"self","q":"On the grid provided, plot a graph of pressure, p (y-axis), against temperature, θ (x-axis). The temperature axis has been drawn from −300 °C to 100 °C. Draw the best-fit straight line through your points.","marks":6,"level":3,"img":{"src":"img/week06/graph_pressure","alt":"On the grid provided, plot a graph of pressure, p (y-axis), against temperature, θ (x-axis). The temperature axis has been drawn from −300 °C to 100 °C. Draw the best-fit straight line through your points.","w":1174,"h":893},"lines":0,"scheme":"All six points plotted correctly to within half a small square (3: −1 per error); best-fit straight line through points (1); line drawn with a ruler, single thin line (1); title (1).","points":[{"text":"All six points plotted correctly to within half a small square","marks":3},{"text":"best-fit straight line through points","marks":1},{"text":"line drawn with a ruler, single thin line","marks":1},{"text":"title","marks":1}],"label":"(a)"},{"id":"week06.past.q1.b","kind":"self","q":"Extend (extrapolate) your line until it cuts the temperature axis. Use it to find a value for absolute zero.","marks":3,"level":2,"lines":2,"scheme":"Line extended as a dashed line to ⟪p=0⟫ (1); value read correctly from the student's line (1); ≈ −270 °C (accept −260 to −285 °C) (1).","points":[{"text":"Line extended as a dashed line to ⟪p=0⟫","marks":1},{"text":"value read correctly from the student's line","marks":1},{"text":"≈ −270 °C (accept −260 to −285 °C)","marks":1}],"num":{"value":-270,"sf":2,"unit":"°C","unitTex":"{}^{\\circ}\\mathrm{C}","min":-285,"max":-260},"label":"(b)","autoKind":"num"},{"id":"week06.past.q1.c","kind":"self","q":"Determine the gradient of your line. Show clearly how you obtained it.","marks":4,"level":3,"lines":4,"scheme":"Large triangle on line (1); correct readings, e.g. ⟪(134-98)/(100-0)⟫ (1); gradient ≈ 0.36 (accept 0.34–0.38) (1); unit kPa °C⁻¹ (1).","points":[{"text":"Large triangle on line","marks":1},{"text":"correct readings, e.g. ⟪(134-98)/(100-0)⟫","marks":1},{"text":"gradient ≈ 0.36 (accept 0.34–0.38)","marks":1},{"text":"unit kPa °C⁻¹","marks":1}],"label":"(c)"},{"id":"week06.past.q1.d","kind":"self","q":"Use your graph to find the pressure at 50 °C. Show on your graph how you obtained it.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":2,"scheme":"Construction lines shown (1); ⟪p\\approx 116\\,\\mathrm{kPa}⟫ (accept 115–117) (1).","points":[{"text":"Construction lines shown","marks":1},{"text":"⟪p\\approx 116\\,\\mathrm{kPa}⟫ (accept 115–117)","marks":1}],"num":{"value":116,"sf":3,"unit":"kPa","unitTex":"\\mathrm{kPa}","min":115,"max":117},"label":"(d)","autoKind":"num"},{"id":"week06.past.q1.e","kind":"self","q":"Using the pressure law and the reading at 0 °C, CALCULATE the pressure at 50 °C. Compare it with your answer to (d).","marks":3,"level":2,"lines":4,"scheme":"⟪T_{1}=273\\,\\mathrm{K}⟫, ⟪T_{2}=323\\,\\mathrm{K}⟫ (1); ⟪p=98\\times 323\\div 273⟫ (1) ⟪=116\\,\\mathrm{kPa}⟫ — agrees with the graph value (1).","points":[{"text":"⟪T_{1}=273\\,\\mathrm{K}⟫, ⟪T_{2}=323\\,\\mathrm{K}⟫","marks":1},{"text":"⟪p=98\\times 323\\div 273⟫","marks":1},{"text":"⟪=116\\,\\mathrm{kPa}⟫ — agrees with the graph value","marks":1}],"label":"(e)"},{"id":"week06.past.q1.f","kind":"self","q":"State TWO precautions the student should take to obtain accurate results.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":3,"scheme":"Any two: flask completely immersed; stir water so uniform temperature; wait for readings to steady before recording; use short/narrow connecting tube; read the thermometer/gauge at eye level (1 each).","points":[{"text":"Any two: flask completely immersed; stir water so uniform temperature; wait for readings to steady before recording; use short/narrow connecting tube; read the thermometer/gauge at eye level (1 each).","marks":2,"each":true}],"label":"(f)"},{"id":"week06.past.q1.g","kind":"self","q":"State why the air in the flask must be dry.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":3,"scheme":"Water vapour would condense/evaporate as the temperature changes (1), changing the mass of gas / so the results would not obey the gas law (1).","points":[{"text":"Water vapour would condense/evaporate as the temperature changes","marks":1},{"text":"changing the mass of gas / so the results would not obey the gas law","marks":1}],"label":"(g)"}]},{"id":"week06.past.q2","kind":"multi","title":"Extended response — thermal energy transfer","total":15,"stem":["Thermal energy can be transferred by conduction, convection and radiation."],"stemBlocks":[],"parts":[{"id":"week06.past.q2.a","kind":"self","q":"Explain how thermal energy is conducted along a metal rod, and why metals conduct heat better than wood.","marks":4,"level":3,"lines":6,"scheme":"Particles at hot end vibrate more vigorously (1); pass energy to neighbouring particles through collisions (1); metals have free electrons (1) which move quickly and carry energy through the metal — wood has no free electrons (1).","points":[{"text":"Particles at hot end vibrate more vigorously","marks":1},{"text":"pass energy to neighbouring particles through collisions","marks":1},{"text":"metals have free electrons","marks":1},{"text":"which move quickly and carry energy through the metal — wood has no free electrons","marks":1}],"label":"(a)"},{"id":"week06.past.q2.b","kind":"self","q":"Describe an experiment to show convection currents in water. Include a labelled diagram, your observations and conclusion.","marks":4,"level":3,"lines":8,"scheme":"Diagram: beaker of water, crystal of potassium permanganate at bottom near one side, heat source under crystal (1); heat gently (1); purple colour rises above crystal, moves across top, sinks on the other side (1); conclusion: heated water becomes less dense and rises, cooler water sinks — convection current (1).","points":[{"text":"Diagram: beaker of water, crystal of potassium permanganate at bottom near one side, heat source under crystal","marks":1},{"text":"heat gently","marks":1},{"text":"purple colour rises above crystal, moves across top, sinks on the other side","marks":1},{"text":"conclusion: heated water becomes less dense and rises, cooler water sinks — convection current","marks":1}],"label":"(b)"},{"id":"week06.past.q2.c","kind":"self","q":"Explain the greenhouse effect and suggest ONE reason why it is a concern for Caribbean countries.","marks":4,"level":3,"lines":6,"scheme":"Short-wave radiation from Sun passes through atmosphere/glass (1); Earth's surface absorbs and emits long-wave infrared (1); CO₂/greenhouse gases absorb/trap this infrared, warming the Earth (1); concern: global warming → sea-level rise / stronger hurricanes / coral bleaching / drought (1).","points":[{"text":"Short-wave radiation from Sun passes through atmosphere/glass","marks":1},{"text":"Earth's surface absorbs and emits long-wave infrared","marks":1},{"text":"CO₂/greenhouse gases absorb/trap this infrared, warming the Earth","marks":1},{"text":"concern: global warming → sea-level rise / stronger hurricanes / coral bleaching / drought","marks":1}],"label":"(c)"},{"id":"week06.past.q2.d","kind":"self","q":"A weather balloon contains 2.0 m^3^ of helium at 100 kPa and 27 °C. It rises to a height where the pressure is 50 kPa and the temperature is −23 °C. Calculate its new volume.","marks":3,"level":2,"lines":4,"scheme":"⟪T_{1}=300\\,\\mathrm{K}⟫, ⟪T_{2}=250\\,\\mathrm{K}⟫ (1); ⟪V_{2}=p_{1}V_{1}T_{2}/(p_{2}T_{1})=100\\times 2.0\\times 250\\div (50\\times 300)⟫ (1) ⟪=3.3\\,\\mathrm{m}^{3}\\ (3.33)⟫ (1).","points":[{"text":"⟪T_{1}=300\\,\\mathrm{K}⟫, ⟪T_{2}=250\\,\\mathrm{K}⟫","marks":1},{"text":"⟪V_{2}=p_{1}V_{1}T_{2}/(p_{2}T_{1})=100\\times 2.0\\times 250\\div (50\\times 300)⟫","marks":1},{"text":"⟪=3.3\\,\\mathrm{m}^{3}\\ (3.33)⟫","marks":1}],"num":{"value":3.3,"sf":2,"unit":"m^3^","unitTex":"\\mathrm{m}^{3}","alt":[3.33]},"label":"(d)","autoKind":"num"}]}],"n":46}]};
